export const murals = [
  {id:1,a:'Aaron Tullo',t:'',loc:'253 2nd Ave N',bldg:'Cordova Inn',lat:27.773992,lng:-82.636422,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'aaron.tullo',bio:'Aaron Tullo is a St. Petersburg-based muralist, sign painter, and fine artist who operates under the Aaron James Tullo Fine Art studio. His work blends figurative realism with bold graphic design, drawing on his background in both traditional painting and commercial art — including work for clients such as St. Pete College, Penn Fishing Reels, and Old Northeast Jewelers. A former minor league baseball player, Tullo channels his travels and community connections into story-driven public art that celebrates place and people. He participated in the 2025 SHINE Origins festival, painting a five-story mural on the Cordova Inn, the festival\'s title sponsor.',desc:'This five-story mural on the facade of the Cordova Inn depicts a large white peacock rendered in Tullo\'s characteristic blend of figurative realism and bold graphic composition. The bird\'s plumage fills the building\'s vertical expanse, creating a striking visual landmark in downtown St. Petersburg.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/aaron-tullo.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:2,a:'Amy Ilic-Volpe',t:'Here & Now',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762931,lng:-82.674592,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'amy_ilic_volpe',bio:'Amy Ilic-Volpe is a St. Petersburg-based multimedia artist whose work explores the interplay of color, form, and perception. Her bold, graphic style uses bright palettes and layered geometric compositions to create work that feels both playful and contemplative. She was the 2022 Gasparilla Festival of the Arts Emerging Artist Recipient, a featured artist in All She Makes magazine, and a SHINE St. Petersburg "Bright Spot" artist in 2022. For the 2025 SHINE Origins festival she returned with a full-scale mural on 31st St & 7th Ave S.',desc:'A wide, vibrant mural on a cinder block wall bursting with overlapping geometric shapes — circles, stripes, polka dots, and waves — in a candy- bright palette of pink, green, yellow, orange, purple, and turquoise. The left side features the words "Here & Now" in stacked multicolor block letters. Signed "Amy Ilic Volpe @amy_ilic_volpe" in pink in the lower right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/amy-ilic-volpe.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:3,a:'Brain The Genius',t:'Culture',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762931,lng:-82.674592,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'brainthegenius',bio:'Chris Roberts, known as BRAIN The Genius (formerly Brain Storm), is a St. Petersburg multidisciplinary artist, tattoo artist, and entrepreneur. He has spent over two decades building Urban Ink Tattoo Company into one of the city\'s most respected studios, recognized as one of the rare Black-owned businesses in the body art community. Beyond tattooing, he is an accomplished painter, designer, and cinematographer who founded Culture Creative, a dynamic art and event space on 34th Street South that amplifies local talent. He painted a SHINE Bright Spot mural in 2020 at the St. Pete Youth Farm and returned for the 2025 SHINE Origins festival.',desc:'A bold mural on a cinder block wall featuring the word "Culture" in ornate gold script, framed by oversized gold bamboo hoop earrings and topped with a gold crown. Lush monstera leaves fill the background in deep greens against black. The left side reads "Bamboo Earrings At Least Two Pair" in white graffiti-style lettering — a nod to the LL Cool J lyric. Signed "BRAIN @brainthegenius" in the lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/brain-the-genius.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:4,a:'Cecilia Lueza',t:'',loc:'251 10th St N',bldg:'Haddy',lat:27.774508,lng:-82.647736,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'cecilialuezaap',bio:'Cecilia Lueza is an Argentine American artist and sculptor who studied fine arts at the University of La Plata in Buenos Aires. After moving to St. Petersburg over two decades ago, she became known for vibrant public art spanning murals, monumental sculptures, painted rooftops, and even street intersections. Her geometric, nature-inspired style has been shown in galleries throughout the US, South America, Spain, and France. A SHINE veteran, Lueza painted "Common Ground" — an entire intersection — for SHINE 2016, "The Blue Hour" for SHINE 2018, and returned again for SHINE 2019 and 2025 Origins.',desc:'A large-scale mural on the side of a commercial building depicting a vividly colored hummingbird in flight, wings spread wide, rendered in iridescent greens, blues, and oranges. The bird hovers against a dynamic background of overlapping translucent circles in gradients of blue, teal, green, and yellow — Lueza\'s signature geometric style. Signed "LUEZA" in the upper right. The building number 255 is visible at the left edge.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/cecilia-lueza.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:5,a:'Derek Donnelly',t:'',loc:'1847 1st Ave N',bldg:'',lat:27.7724559,lng:-82.658692,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'saintpaintarts',bio:'Derek Donnelly, known as Saint Paint Arts, is a St. Petersburg native whose cartoon-influenced murals have become landmarks across Tampa Bay. A multiple Best of the Bay winner from Creative Loafing Tampa Bay, Donnelly\'s playful, story-driven style blends retro illustration with local culture — often featuring his son and the city\'s wildlife. His large-scale work spans hotels, breweries, public buildings, and private homes throughout the region. For the 2025 SHINE Origins festival, he painted two murals: this piece at 1847 1st Ave N and a vintage postcard companion at the Hollander Hotel.',desc:'A wide-format mural wrapping around two walls of a single-story building. The central scene shows Donnelly\'s signature cartoon boy — in a red cap and sunglasses — riding a pelican in flight over ocean waves. A dragonfly hovers on the right side, and seagulls fill the sky. The left wall transitions into an underwater scene in deep blue-green tones, with a diver amid coral and sea life. Signed "Donnelly" in the upper right. The palette shifts from cool oceanic blues on the left to bright sky blue and green on the right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/derek-donnelly-1847.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:6,a:'Derek Donnelly',t:'Greetings from Hollander Hotel',loc:'421 4th Ave N',bldg:'Hollander Hotel',lat:27.776731,lng:-82.638764,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'saintpaintarts',bio:'Derek Donnelly, known as Saint Paint Arts, is a St. Petersburg native whose cartoon-influenced murals have become landmarks across Tampa Bay. A multiple Best of the Bay winner from Creative Loafing Tampa Bay, Donnelly\'s playful, story-driven style blends retro illustration with local culture — often featuring his son and the city\'s wildlife. His large-scale work spans hotels, breweries, public buildings, and private homes throughout the region. For the 2025 SHINE Origins festival, he painted two murals: this vintage postcard piece at the Hollander Hotel and a companion work at 1847 1st Ave N.',desc:'A vintage-style postcard mural reading "Greetings from Hollander Hotel, St. Pete, FL." A cartoon boy in a red cap rides a pelican wearing sunglasses over turquoise waters, with dolphins leaping below, seagulls overhead, and a palm-tree island with the Sunshine Skyway Bridge in the distance. Signed "Donnelly @Saint Paint Arts" in the lower right. The retro color palette of teals, creams, and warm tans gives it a classic Florida tourism feel.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/derek-donnelly-hollander.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:7,a:'Dreamweaver',t:'',loc:'696 1st Ave N',bldg:'Vernis Bowling',lat:27.771708,lng:-82.643617,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'dream.weavin',bio:'Taylor "Dreamweaver" Smith is a St. Petersburg-based street artist and muralist whose work invokes a sense of reverence for the natural world. With a background in fine art, her evolving style integrates expressive abstraction and realism to capture the spirit of life through light and color — often featuring portraiture, vivid patterns, and striking color combinations. She creates murals, canvas works, and live painting activations for communities and brands both locally and nationwide. A recurring SHINE artist, Dreamweaver painted at the 2022 festival and returned for 2025 Origins on the 600 block of Central Avenue, where the SHINE movement started in 2012.',desc:'A multi-story mural wrapping around a lavender building, featuring a white egret with delicate plumage nestled among enormous, lush flower petals in deep pinks, corals, oranges, and purples. The bird\'s long neck rises elegantly through the blooms on the left wall, while the right wall continues with swirling petals and soft round forms in warm sunset tones. The scale is dramatic — the egret spans several stories — and the soft-focus, almost photorealistic rendering gives the whole piece a dreamlike, ethereal quality.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/dreamweaver.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL'},
  {id:8,a:'Elizabeth Barenis',t:'',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762931,lng:-82.674592,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'elizabethbarenis',bio:'Born and raised in Greenville, Mississippi, Elizabeth Barenis has been painting since childhood. A graduate of Sarah Lawrence College in New York, she was named a 2023 Creative Pinellas Professional Artist grantee and participated in the 2018 International Painting Symposium at the Mark Rothko Art Center in Latvia. Her signature "Abstract Precisionism" style, inspired by her photography, uses softly abstracted shapes and colors to evoke tranquility and beauty. Now based in Pinellas County, she believes "it\'s the artist\'s job to amplify" beauty and that "art for a community really uplifts the community." She participated in the 2025 SHINE Origins festival.',desc:'A wide mural on a cinder block wall depicting oversized tropical fan palm fronds in layered shades of green, teal, and soft yellow. The leaves radiate outward in clean, geometric planes — Barenis\'s "Abstract Precisionism" style — creating depth through overlapping translucent shapes. Signed "Elizabeth Barenis 2025" in the lower left corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/elizabeth-barenis.jpeg',from:'Pinellas County, FL'},
  {id:118,a:'Elizabeth Barenis',t:'Healing Palms',loc:'744 6th Ave. S.',bldg:'Bayfront Health St. Pete',lat:27.764375,lng:-82.64328,y:2025,cat:'commercial',ig:'',bio:'Elizabeth Barenis is a St. Petersburg-based painter originally from Greenville, Mississippi, who studied at Sarah Lawrence College in New York. She works in a style she calls "Abstract Precisionism" — gently abstracted shapes and colors drawn from her photography, intended to recreate moments of tranquility. She was named a 2023 Creative Pinellas Professional Artist grantee and participated in the International Painting Symposium at the Mark Rothko Art Center in Latvia in 2018. "Healing Palms" was commissioned by Orlando Health as an original painting, then enlarged and printed on the louvered exterior wall of the Bayfront Hospital ambulance entrance. The installation translates her tropical foliage compositions to architectural scale, wrapping the building in overlapping palm fronds rendered in cool greens and blues.',desc:'Overlapping angular planes in shades of green and blue fill the louvered exterior wall of the Bayfront Health ambulance entrance. The geometric shapes suggest tropical palm fronds abstracted into faceted, crystalline forms — a diptych painting enlarged to building scale. The cool palette and precise geometry complement the medical campus architecture while adding a distinctly tropical warmth.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/unknown-1498.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:9,a:'Isac Gres',t:'',loc:'699 Central Ave',bldg:'Melting Pot',lat:27.771678,lng:-82.643892,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'isacgres.art',bio:'Isac Gres (b. 1993, Cuba) is a surrealist painter, muralist, and tattoo artist whose work transforms themes of exile, memory, and resilience into dreamlike imagery. After a 12-country journey to the U.S., he developed a symbolic language of doorways, jellyfish, and apples — metaphors of identity and transformation. His large-scale work blends hyperrealistic technique with cosmic surrealism, often featuring a recurring motif of beaming light that he describes as representing "light and good energy." For the 2025 SHINE Origins festival, Gres took on his tallest wall to date on the 600 block of Central Avenue — where the mural movement that launched SHINE began in 2012 — alongside Dreamweaver and John Vitale.',desc:'A massive two-story mural wrapping around the corner of a building in swirling cosmic surrealism. The composition features molten planetary spheres, a city skyline encased in a dripping glass orb, melting candles nodding to Dalí, and a sculptural elephant form emerging from fiery nebula clouds. The palette is dominated by deep space blues and purples contrasted with blazing oranges, reds, and golds. Signed "Isac Gres" in the lower corners of both walls.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/isac-gres.jpeg',from:'Cuba / St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:10,a:'Jenipher Chandley',t:'Stronger Together',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762867,lng:-82.675103,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'jenipherchandley',bio:'Jenipher Chandley is a St. Petersburg-based representational artist whose work explores the relationship between nature and the human condition, focusing on themes of strength, beauty, loss, and resilience. Working predominantly with oils, she creates figurative artwork infused with surrealism and symbolism. She holds degrees in Interior and Graphic Design and has shown her work in hundreds of shows and charitable events while curating exhibitions throughout Tampa Bay. A 2023 Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist grantee, she has also been selected as the Local Artist Spotlight by the Gasparilla Festival of the Arts and featured by American Stage Theater. She participated in SHINE 2021 and returned for SHINE Origins 2025.',desc:'A wide mural on a cinder block wall with the words "Stronger Together" in large flowing white script across a deep teal background. The text is framed by a lush border of curling green vines and tropical flame-colored flowers — gloriosa lilies in reds, oranges, and yellows. Signed "Jenipher Chandley" in the upper right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/jenipher-chandley.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:11,a:'John Vitale',t:'Power to the Patients',loc:'600 1st Ave N',bldg:'',lat:27.7716472,lng:-82.6446195,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'vitalebros',bio:'John Vitale, co-founder of the iconic Vitale Brothers, is a veteran muralist and creative force behind much of St. Petersburg\'s visual identity. Since 1992, the Vitale Bros have painted walls throughout Tampa Bay, playing a major role in establishing St. Petersburg as a world-class arts destination. John is also the founder of Overspray Art Space in Gulfport, where he fosters community through spray paint workshops and live art events. With over three decades of experience painting large-scale public works, he combines classic craftsmanship with bold, contemporary design. A recurring SHINE artist, he painted at SHINE 2022 and returned for SHINE Origins 2025 on the 600 block where the mural movement began.',desc:'A propaganda-style mural in the tradition of Shepard Fairey\'s OBEY aesthetic. A raised fist gripping a caduceus medical symbol is centered in a bold red circle, with "Power to the Patients" in block letters below. The top reads "Prices Are Now a Patient\'s Right" and the bottom banner declares "Demand Them!!!" in large cream type on red. The palette is limited to navy blue, red, cream, and gray — evoking classic activist poster art. "PowerToThePatients.org" runs along the bottom, with Vitale Bros logos flanking it.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/john-vitale-600.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:12,a:'John Vitale & Sionna MacLeish',t:'',loc:'2606 Fairfield Ave S',bldg:'The Factory',lat:27.763314,lng:-82.668853,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'vitalebros',bio:'John Vitale, co-founder of the iconic Vitale Brothers, is a veteran muralist and creative force behind much of St. Petersburg\'s visual identity. Since 1992, the Vitale Bros have painted walls throughout Tampa Bay, playing a major role in establishing St. Petersburg as a world-class arts destination. John is also the founder of Overspray Art Space in Gulfport, where he fosters community through spray paint workshops and live art events. For this mural at The Factory, Vitale collaborated with Sionna "Sio" MacLeish, a Florida-based muralist and artist known for her versatility across styles. MacLeish painted the roseate spoonbill panel, while Vitale handled the vintage pin-up and Thunderbird motel scene.',desc:'A multi-panel vintage Florida postcard mural inside The Factory. The left panel features a glamorous 1950s-style pin-up woman with black hair and a pink hibiscus, turquoise jewelry, and a leopard-print top, set against the Thunderbird motel sign framed by tropical hibiscus flowers. Below reads "St. Petersburg, Florida" in retro blue script on golden yellow. The right panel, painted by Sionna MacLeish, shows a roseate spoonbill in soft pinks against a pale blue polka-dot background with a bold orange sun. The whole composition evokes mid-century Florida tourism nostalgia.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/john-vitale-factory.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:13,a:'Karel Garcia',t:'',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762931,lng:-82.674478,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'karelgarcia.fineartist',bio:'Karel Garcia (b. 1990, Vedado, Cuba) trained as an architect in Havana, completing his degree in 2017. While working as an architect, he reconnected with art through exhibitions including projects at the Habana Espacio Creativo Center, developing his "Human" series exploring the human experience. After emigrating to Florida, he joined the Green Book of Tampa Bay residency program with a studio in St. Petersburg\'s Warehouse Arts District. The 2025 SHINE Origins festival marked Garcia\'s first public art installation in America. "My mural is about my history, coming here to America, looking for freedom. So it\'s about migration, love, freedom, family, and live your truth," he said.',desc:'A wide mural on a cinder block wall depicting bold, swirling ocean waves in deep blues and greens against a golden-orange sky. Three carved wooden totem-like hands rise from the waves, and small red paper boats ride the cresting swells — symbols of migration and hope. The flowing, graphic linework gives the waves a stylized, rhythmic energy. Signed "Karel Garcia" in the lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/karel-garcia.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:128,a:'Raisa Nosova',t:'Woman of Light',loc:'2606 Fairfield Ave S',bldg:'Museum of Motherhood (The Factory)',lat:27.76383833,lng:-82.66821333,y:2025,cat:'commercial',ig:'raisanosova',bio:'Raisa Nosova is a Russian-born multidisciplinary artist working in painting, murals, blown glass sculpture, drawing, animation, and performance art. She earned her MFA from NYU Steinhardt and her BFA from the Fashion Institute of Technology, and studied blown glass at Urban Glass in Brooklyn. She also attended Vesalius College in Brussels and the Art Students League of New York. Her experience of becoming a mother during her MFA profoundly shifted her practice toward themes of motherhood, trauma, resilience, displacement, and the invisible labor of caregiving — she was two months pregnant when Russia bombed Kyiv, and with close family in Ukraine, that event also shaped her work. She\'s best known for blown glass breast sculptures exploring postpartum experience, and her anti-war mural "Uprooted" on a Ukrainian- owned gas station in Jersey City. She\'s exhibited at Theodore Art Gallery in Manhattan, La Galerie Emeric Hahn in Paris, and Mana Contemporary in Jersey City, and has held residencies at the Berlin Art Institute and the Ramona Residency in Houston (the first artist-mother residency in Texas). She has over 15 years of teaching experience, including adjunct professorships at NYU and FIT.',desc:'A dynamic, warm-toned mural on the facade of the Museum of Motherhood at The Factory. A female figure is depicted in a crouching or seated position, rendered in expressive brushwork in reds, golds, pinks, and deep magentas. Radiating golden light beams and architectural fragments burst outward from behind the figure, creating a sense of energy and movement. The composition has a stained-glass quality with bold color blocks and strong outlines. The "MoM" (Museum of Motherhood) text is visible in the upper left. The piece wraps around the building entrance, framing the doorway.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/raisa-nosova-museum-of-motherhood.jpeg',from:'Hoboken, NJ'},
  {id:15,a:'Rebekah Lazaridis',t:'',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762931,lng:-82.674544,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'rebekahlaz',bio:'Rebekah Eugenia Lazaridis is a St. Pete native and multidisciplinary artist who graduated with a BFA from The School of the Art Institute of Chicago in 2005. A scenic artist and designer, she has created sets for productions at American Stage, Jobsite, freeFall, and Stageworks. She also runs @homeillustrations, creating customized home portraits with a nostalgic flair, and her CIRCA series portrays iconic St. Petersburg spots in a vintage postcard style. Her first NYC solo show explored theatrical superstitions. She participated in SHINE 2025 Origins at 31st St & 7th Ave S.',desc:'A dramatic monochromatic mural on a black cinder block wall. A large ornate Gothic letter "B" with intricate Celtic knotwork fills the center, overlaid on a detailed full moon rendered in grays and whites. Bats swarm from the left across the night sky, and a barn owl with outstretched wings emerges from storm clouds on the right, with rain streaking diagonally downward. Signed "ReAh @rebekahlaz" in the lower right corner. The entire palette is black, white, and silver — a stark contrast to the colorful murals flanking it.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/rebekah-lazaridis.jpeg',from:'Pinellas County, FL'},
  {id:16,a:'Reid Jenkins',t:'',loc:'2039 1st Ave S',bldg:'Founders Pro',lat:27.770058,lng:-82.661369,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'nighttakesking',bio:'Reid Jenkins is a St. Petersburg native who holds a BFA from Maryland Institute College of Art and is a graduate of the P.C.C.A program at Gibbs High School. His work spans murals, canvas paintings, and tattoos — he currently tattoos at Fabled Trade Tattoo Company in Lealman and maintains a studio at the Arts Exchange in the Warehouse Arts District. Jenkins aims to paint the human experience and capture the small moments that many forget to appreciate. He painted a SHINE mural in 2021 and returned for SHINE Origins 2025 with this botanical piece at Founders Pro.',desc:'A stunning photorealistic botanical mural on the side of a commercial building against a deep black background. An overflowing arrangement of tropical flowers fills the wall — king proteas, red ginger, heliconia, pincushion proteas, orchids, and ferns rendered in rich crimsons, pinks, corals, golds, and greens. The hyperrealistic detail and dramatic dark background give it the feel of a Dutch Golden Age still life painting scaled up to building size.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/reid-jenkins.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:17,a:'Sara Salem',t:'We Are St. Pete',loc:'2180 4th St N',bldg:'LMCU',lat:27.792028,lng:-82.638969,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'sarasalemartsy',bio:'Sara Salem is a St. Petersburg-based mural artist and co-founder of Phi Phi Artland, a creative arts center blending fine art, wellness, and community. She holds a BFA (2011) and brings over 12 years of teaching experience spanning Africa, Asia, and the USA. Beyond murals, Salem is a yoga instructor, Reiki Level II practitioner, and certified art therapist who co-founded Milky Way Retreats. For the 2025 SHINE Origins festival, she led a community mural project at LMCU Bank, inviting local residents to help paint alongside her — one of the festival\'s signature collaborative highlights.',desc:'A large mural on the side of a building featuring a colorful pelican wearing a white baseball cap and reflective sunglasses that show St. Pete landmarks in their lenses. The pelican\'s wings are spread wide in vibrant pinks, oranges, blues, and deep reds. A golden spiral sun radiates in the upper left corner, and "We Are St. Pete" appears in playful navy blue script to the right. Signed "Phi Phi Artland @Sarasalemartsy" at the bottom. The white background keeps the whole piece bright and inviting.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/sara-salem.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:19,a:'Zulu Painter',t:'',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762869,lng:-82.674928,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'zulupainter1',bio:'Carlos Culbertson, known as Zulu Painter, is a St. Petersburg-based multimedia artist who works in traditional mediums, body paint, special effects, and scenic art. His motto — "I want to grow Art everywhere" — drives him as he creates "Mindful Murals" across the country. He has worked as a scenic artist for The Science Channel, Disney on Ice, Home Shopping Network, Sea World, and national ad campaigns, and served as a Representative Artist for Visit Florida. A SHINE veteran since 2016, his murals are especially prominent in the Deuces neighborhood, including "Moonbeam Dreams" and "Wisdom, Growth, Progress." He returned for SHINE 2017, 2018, 2024, and 2025 Origins.',desc:'A vibrant mural on a cinder block wall blending multiple styles. The left side features a photorealistic portrait of a young Black woman in profile against a houndstooth pattern with a boombox rendered in grayscale. The center and right side shift to a cartoon aesthetic — a wild-eyed character with a mischievous grin faces off against a massive green alligator with bared teeth, set against a turquoise geometric diamond pattern and black- and-white checkerboard. Colorful polka dots in orange, pink, and yellow punctuate the composition. Signed "zulupainter.com" in the lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/zulu-painter.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:20,a:'Abys',t:'',loc:'201 17th Street South',bldg:'Modera',lat:27.768439,lng:-82.658011,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'abys_osmoz',bio:'Julien Brouand, known as Abys, is a self-taught French artist from Nancy, born in 1991. Passionate about drawing and cartoons from a young age, he discovered graffiti in 2009. After years practicing with various collectives, he developed his own character-driven style in 2015 — staging lively, narrative, and colorful compositions mixing whimsical characters, decorations, and lettering. For the 2024 SHINE festival, he painted this six-story gator in just four days and had enough time and paint left over to create a bonus crab mural on the back of Anchor Skate Supply nearby.',desc:'A six-story-tall mural on the side of the Modera apartment building depicting a cartoonish, pot-bellied alligator with a mischievous grin, riding a red surfboard through splashing turquoise waves. A pink hibiscus flower sits atop the gator\'s head, and teal smoke curls above. The surfboard reads "SHINE" in stylized lettering. At the base, a triangular logo anchors the composition. The character\'s exaggerated features and vibrant green-on-gray color scheme make it visible from blocks away.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/abys.jpeg',from:'Nancy, France'},
  {id:22,a:'Dwayne Shepherd & Brian McAllister',t:'The Importance of ART Education',loc:'3535 Central Ave',bldg:'Bell Pharmacy',lat:27.771228,lng:-82.681511,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'dwayneshepherd3499',bio:'Dwayne Shepherd is a visual arts teacher at Osceola Middle School in Seminole, FL, where he has taught for over 22 years and founded a mural club that transformed campus walls with student-painted murals. A graduate of Austin Peay State University, he was named Florida Art Teacher of the Year in 2015 and has been a Pinellas County Teacher of the Year finalist three times. Brian McAllister is an international muralist, illustrator, and visual arts instructor at the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School. A Ringling College of Art and Design graduate, he founded the Gibbs Mural Club, a 13-year community outreach program with 36 students creating professional public art. His SHINE credits include the 133-foot "Great Futures Start Here" mural on the historic Royal Theater in 2019. Together, Shepherd and McAllister collaborate with art educators statewide through the Florida Art Education Association (FAEA).',desc:'A vibrant marine-themed mural on the side of Bell Pharmacy celebrating art education. The composition centers on an underwater scene with sea turtles swimming among mangrove roots, tropical fish, and coral. Dolphins arc through the water above while the text "The Importance of ART Education" anchors the design. The mural was painted collaboratively with art educators from across Florida during the FAEA annual conference in October 2024 as one of four "Bright Spots" community murals in SHINE\'s 10th anniversary festival.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/dwayne-shepherd-brian-mcallister.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:23,a:'Emmanuel Jarus',t:'',loc:'226 5th Ave N',bldg:'Walker-Whitney',lat:27.777247,lng:-82.636847,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'youngjarus',bio:'Emmanuel Jarus (b. 1992) is a Canadian muralist and figurative painter based in Toronto. Originally from Saskatoon, Saskatchewan, he transitioned from painting freight trains to large-scale murals around age 19. Largely self-taught with brief studies at the Academy of Realist Art in Toronto, his style blends post-impressionistic influences with unconventional hardware-store materials — acrylic, latex, aerosol, brushes, rollers, and sometimes a broom. He describes his approach as "painting journalism," creating portraiture that emerges organically from the communities he visits. His international credits include the first UN75 "Zero Hunger" mural near United Nations headquarters in Manhattan (2020), festivals in Boulogne-sur-Mer, Calgary, Halifax, and Toronto\'s Graffiti Alley. He is represented by LALA Contemporary gallery.',desc:'A 14-story photorealistic portrait of an enigmatic woman in flowing black garments spanning the entire west-facing wall of the Walker-Whitney building — the largest mural in SHINE\'s 10-year history at roughly 180 feet tall by 60 feet wide. The woman has long brown hair and one hand resting on her chest, gazing out with a serene, mysterious expression against a muted gray-teal background. Jarus intentionally keeps her identity a secret. The figure is a recurring subject he first painted on a seaside structure in Boulogne-sur-Mer, France. Painted with brushes, rollers, and bucket paint over 20 days — interrupted by Hurricane Milton\'s evacuation — in time for SHINE\'s 10th anniversary festival in November 2024.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/emmanuel-jarus.jpeg',from:'Toronto, Canada'},
  {id:24,a:'Fintan Magee',t:'',loc:'777 3rd Ave North',bldg:'Reflection St. Pete',lat:27.77525833,lng:-82.64443833,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'fintan_magee',bio:'Fintan Magee is an Australian muralist based in Sydney, known for large-scale social realist paintings that tackle environmental themes — water, endangered species, the refugee crisis. He holds a BFA from Griffith University and started as a graffiti writer before shifting to figurative public art. His walls appear in cities from London and Vienna to Buenos Aires and Kyiv, and he\'s shown at Nuart Festival in Stavanger and Aberdeen. His SHINE 2024 mural spans four stories of the Reflection St. Pete apartment building, depicting an underwater scene in cool blues and teals — a submerged figure, a glass bottle, crystal formations, and a pineapple, all rendered with photorealistic precision. The piece plays on the water and submersion themes that run through much of Magee\'s environmental work.',desc:'A four-story mural in shades of blue and teal covers the face of the Reflection St. Pete building. The scene depicts objects and a figure submerged underwater — a glass perfume-style bottle, crystalline formations, and a pineapple float in rippling blue water. The photorealistic rendering captures light refracting through the water surface above. The building\'s "REFLECTION" signage sits just below the mural\'s lower edge.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/fintan-magee.jpeg',from:'Australia'},
  {id:25,a:'Frankie Gonzalez',t:'',loc:'2606 Fairfield Ave South',bldg:'FloridaRAMA',lat:27.763367,lng:-82.668428,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'frankieg_art',bio:'Francisco "Frankie G" Gonzalez is a Miami-born, Tampa-based mixed-media artist whose work blends comic, urban, and traditional painting techniques into bold, culture-infused compositions rooted in his Cuban and Danish heritage. After attending the Atlanta College of Art, he spent nine years in Salt Lake City before returning to Florida to pursue art full-time. He combines spray paint and latex to create figurative subjects juxtaposed with street art and pop art elements. His public commissions include "Tampa\'s LIGHT" at Tampa Fire Rescue Headquarters and projects for the cities of Tampa, Clearwater, Largo, and St. Petersburg. Commercial clients include ESPN, the Tampa Bay Buccaneers, Norwegian Cruise Lines, and Ducati. He also runs mural camps in local schools and works with Habitat for Humanity and the Down Syndrome Foundation of Florida.',desc:'A wide, energetic mural on the side of FloridaRAMA blending photorealism with street art. At center, a boy in a baseball cap and tan shirt launches a paper airplane, his outstretched arm dissolving into bold black calligraphic strokes and cartoon-style characters. Oversized pink peonies, sunflowers, and white daisies bloom along the bottom, while swirls of pink, yellow, orange, and turquoise fill the background. The composition moves from lush botanical realism on the left through the figurative center to playful abstraction on the right, creating a dynamic sense of childhood imagination taking flight.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/frankie-gonzalez.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL'},
  {id:26,a:'George F Baker III',t:'At Your Show',loc:'620 1st Ave South',bldg:'Studio@620',lat:27.77008833,lng:-82.64189167,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'gfb3',bio:'George F. Baker III (GFB3) is a Nebraska-born, Detroit-raised illustrator and muralist who has called Atlanta home since his college years. He earned his BFA from Georgia State University in 2015 and quickly built a reputation for chunky, candy-colored characters packed with infectious energy. His client list reads like a brand hall of fame — Nike, Jordan Brand, ESPN, Mercedes-Benz, and Levi\'s among them — and he has painted more than 50 large-scale murals across the U.S. He gave a TEDxAtlanta talk on creativity and community, earned a nomination for the World Illustration Awards, and co-founded Foster, a multidisciplinary studio-collective in Atlanta where he serves as Creative Director. Whether on a building or a sneaker box, his work channels a playful, childlike spirit meant to remind people of who they really want to be.',desc:'A full-building mural on a black background packed with a joyful crowd of cartoon-style characters rendered in bold pinks, teals, purples, blues, and warm browns. The figures are chunky and expressive with exaggerated grins, wild hair, and animated poses — dancing, laughing, taking selfies, playing instruments, and celebrating. Across the top in large bubble letters with teal outlines and cream fill reads "WE\'RE ALL FRONT ROW AT YOUR SHOW." White diamond-shaped dots scatter across the black background like confetti or stars. The GFB3 tag appears in a small heart-shaped logo in the lower right corner. The piece wraps around garage doors and windows, covering the entire rear wall of Studio@620.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/george-f-baker-iii.jpeg',from:'Atlanta, GA'},
  {id:27,a:'Kris Markovich & Johnny Vitale',t:'',loc:'501 16th St S',bldg:'Anchor Skate Supply',lat:27.76560333,lng:-82.65497,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'kris_markovich',bio:'Kris Markovich turned pro for Dogtown Skateboards in 1990 and spent three decades riding for 14 different board sponsors — including 101, Foundation, Blind, and Element — earning Transworld covers, filming parts in major videos, and touring with Tony Hawk. He started painting in the late \'90s, first on skate decks, then walls. His art zeroes in on eyes and faces rendered in a raw, expressive style. He now rides for Anchor Skate Supply in St. Pete and runs Black Griptape Company. His largest mural is at Harrah\'s Casino in San Diego, and he\'s painted walls from Augusta, Maine to Dubai. Johnny Vitale co-founded the Vitale Bros in 1992 with brother Paul, following a family tradition started by their grandparents. They began by hand-lettering signs and painting storefronts — their first mural came from a napkin sketch pitched to a restaurant owner. Over 30+ years they\'ve become St. Pete\'s most prolific muralists, painting for the Tampa Bay Rays, Buccaneers, and L\'Oreal\'s SalonCentric nationally. They also did set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers. Johnny now runs Overspray Art Space in Gulfport with graffiti workshops and rotating gallery shows. This SHINE 2024 collaboration drew inspiration from the Tampa Bay Rays\' City Connect uniforms, which were themselves inspired by St. Pete\'s skate and street art culture.',desc:'A bold, graphic mural wrapping around the entire exterior of Anchor Skate Supply. The building is painted in a black base with vibrant tropical and tiki-inspired imagery. A large pink and green sugar skull or tiki mask dominates the left side, while cartoon-style palm trees and waves in neon green and teal sweep across the front. A character with sunglasses peers over the roofline. The right side features more black-and-white tribal/tiki patterns and the text "OVER SPRAY ART COLLECTIVE." The whole building reads as a single immersive artwork, turning the skate shop into a neighborhood landmark.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/kris-markovich-johnny-vitale.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:29,a:'NeSpoon',t:'',loc:'601 24th St South',bldg:'Cemex',lat:27.764411,lng:-82.666214,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'nes.nespoon',bio:'NeSpoon (Elzbieta Dymna) is a Polish street artist based in Warsaw whose signature medium is lace — traditional textile patterns rendered at monumental scale on building facades and pressed into ceramic pieces installed on streets. Her artist name comes from the famous line in The Matrix. She began her street art career in 2009 after discovering that pressing lace into clay was a traditional decorative technique, and has since created work in over 100 cities across 40 countries on five continents. At each location she researches local lace-making traditions through museums and community conversations. Her work has appeared at the Louvre-Lens, EXPO Dubai 2020, the European Parliament in Brussels, and the Urban Nation Biennale in Berlin. She won the ARTAQ Prize in France in 2011 and 2012 and was selected for Urban Nation\'s Fresh A.I.R. residency in Berlin in 2018.',desc:'A wide mural on the Cemex building featuring NeSpoon\'s signature lace patterns in white and pale blue against a deep plum-purple background. Intricate medallions with floral and geometric motifs occupy the left and center, their delicate threadwork rendered with illusory three-dimensional depth through careful shading and layering. The right side transitions into flowing, organic swirls that evoke wind or waves. The effect transforms the industrial concrete wall into what appears to be an enormous draped lace textile. Signed "NeSpoon" in the lower left corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/nespoon.jpeg',from:'Warsaw, Poland'},
  {id:30,a:'Quinn Cale',t:'',loc:'225 2nd Ave North',bldg:'Main Grove',lat:27.77370833,lng:-82.6362,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'quinnsessential',bio:'Quinn Cale is a Tampa-based painter originally from Delaware whose work channels early-2000s childhood nostalgia into bold, colorful compositions. She previously worked at Apple, an experience that led her to experiment with augmented reality overlays on her paintings using Adobe Aero — viewers can point an iPad at the canvas and see digital elements come to life. Her SHINE 2024 mural at Main Grove features a multicolored cat in vivid neon hues pouncing toward a pixelated mouse cursor and a retro-styled pixel hand pointer. The gaming references and rainbow palette are pure Cale — pop culture nostalgia meets street art energy.',desc:'A large cat rendered in electric purples, teals, oranges, and pinks leaps across the wall, chasing a pixelated orange mouse and a retro cursor hand icon. The cat\'s fur is broken into bold color blocks that read like a neon stained-glass window. The whole piece pops against a dark teal and sage background.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/quinn-cale.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL'},
  {id:31,a:'Stevie Shao',t:'',loc:'850 5th Ave South',bldg:'Volunteers of America',lat:27.765203,lng:-82.646683,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'stepfrae',bio:'Stevie Shao is a second-generation Chinese-American illustrator and muralist born and raised in Seattle. She studied interior design at Seattle Pacific University, where an illustration course with Rachell Sumpter sparked her artistic path. After being laid off during COVID-19 in 2020, she transitioned to full-time art, quickly becoming one of Seattle\'s most in-demand public artists. Her style blends folk art, Chinese ceramics, and traditional American tattoo influences into ornate, maximalist compositions filled with plants, animals, and mythical creatures. Clients include Starbucks, REI, Google, Nordstrom, Sub Pop Records, and the Seattle Kraken, for whom she designed the 2022 Women in Hockey jersey. Fashion designer Anna Sui featured her work in her Spring/Summer 2021 collection. She was named one of Seattle Magazine\'s "25 Most Influential" in 2023.',desc:'A multi-story mural on the side of the Volunteers of America building depicting a sinuous, serpentine dragon in bold navy blue, red, gold, and turquoise against a white background. The dragon coils upward across the full height of the building, its body decorated with pink scalloped scales and chartreuse dots. Two stylized birds with spread wings — one near the top and one at the bottom — intertwine with the dragon\'s twisting form. Blue flames curl from the dragon\'s mouth. The flat, graphic style draws on Chinese folk art and traditional tattoo aesthetics. Signed "Stevie Shao 24" in the lower right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/stevie-shao.jpeg',from:'Seattle, WA'},
  {id:32,a:'Up & Over',t:'',loc:'201 17th Street South',bldg:'Modera',lat:27.76938333,lng:-82.65769167,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'upandovermurals',bio:'Up & Over is the collaboration between Leon "Tes One" Bedore and the Vitale Bros, formed around 2020. Tes One has been painting in St. Pete since 1992, starting as a graffiti writer before building a career that blends traditional art with graphic design for clients like Nike, L\'Oreal, Absolut Vodka, and Architectural Digest. In 2012 he curated the "Leave a Message" show at the Morean Arts Center, which planted the seed for what became the SHINE Mural Festival in 2015 — he co-founded and co-curated the inaugural festival that brought 17 murals to downtown and has since produced nearly 170. He had a full career retrospective at the Morean in 2022 and was featured in the Dali Museum\'s "Outside In" exhibition in 2025. The Vitale Bros — Johnny, Paul, and Joey Vitale — have been painting together since 1992, starting with hand-lettered signs and storefront windows. Their warehouse studio hosted legendary art and music shows in the late \'90s that helped build St. Pete\'s arts scene before it boomed. Together as Up & Over, they\'ve painted murals for the Tampa Bay Buccaneers, Duke Energy, and First Citrus Bank. This SHINE 2024 piece marks a full-circle moment: the festival\'s founder painting alongside the city\'s most prolific muralists for the 10th anniversary.',desc:'A towering abstract geometric mural spanning the full height of the Modera apartment building — roughly five stories. Bold, interlocking organic shapes in saturated primary and secondary colors — red, teal, orange, yellow, deep blue, maroon, and white — overlap and flow across the facade. The forms are rounded and architectural, somewhere between hard-edge abstraction and biomorphic design, with circles nested inside flowing vertical bands. The color relationships are carefully balanced, creating a sense of depth and movement. The scale dominates the block and is visible from several streets away.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/up-and-over.jpeg',from:'Tampa Bay, FL'},
  {id:37,a:'Bryan Beyung & James Lee Chiahan',t:'When the Sun is Up, the Moon is Absent!',loc:'2875 7th Ave S',bldg:'Stewart Moving & Storage',lat:27.762889,lng:-82.673758,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'bryanbeyung',bio:'Bryan Beyung (王永財) is a Sino-Cambodian visual artist born in Montreal to a family of Chinese-Cambodian refugees. He studied graphic design at Cégep Marie-Victorin and color theory and muralism at Galería LaRoja in Seville, Spain. His work explores immigration, diasporic heritage, and migratory flows, blending neo-muralism, post-graffiti, and contemporary painting. Notable projects include "The Sea Keeper," the first mural on a Canadian bulk carrier, and public works across the US, Canada, Haiti, France, Morocco, and Cambodia. James Lee Chiahan (李佳翰, b. 1990) is a Taiwanese-Canadian artist from Tainan, Taiwan, now based in Montreal. Largely self-taught in oil painting, he creates emotionally resonant work exploring memory, closeness, and distance. His editorial illustration clients include The New Yorker, The Atlantic, The Washington Post, The New York Times, and Apple. He won the 2024 Canadian Magazine Awards Grand Prize for Cover Art. The two share a studio in Montreal.',desc:'A two-panel mural on the Stewart Moving & Storage building. The left side features a large black circle on a golden background containing a white tree silhouette and crescent moon — based on a drawing by six-year-old Amelia Kien — with the word "ABSENT" below and colorful childlike figures along the bottom painted by the Kien family. The right side shows a photorealistic sunset landscape with four family members — two adults and two children — standing on a hillside, connected by a red textile panel with traditional patterns. A navy blue band along the base depicts fish silhouettes. The mural tells the story of the Kien family, who fled Cambodia in 1987 and eventually settled near Bradenton, Florida, just minutes from this wall.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/bryan-beyung-james-lee-chiahan.jpeg',from:'Montreal, Canada'},
  {id:38,a:'Bunnie Reiss',t:'Phoebe & Friends',loc:'2700 Central Ave',bldg:'MTBH Studios',lat:27.77075333,lng:-82.67048667,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'bunnieluvrocks',bio:'Bunnie Reiss is a muralist based near Joshua Tree, California who paints massive folk-art walls full of animals, flowers, and dense patterning. She grew up in a Polish/Russian family in Colorado, studied literature there, and worked for NPR before earning her MFA from the San Francisco Art Institute. She spent 20+ years in the Bay Area before relocating to a 5-acre art farm called Lilac Lane Farms in the Mojave Desert town of Landers. Her style draws from Eastern European folk traditions, Mexican folk art, Russian fairy tales, and Jewish mysticism — all filtered through a maximalist color palette. She\'s painted walls in over 20 countries and has had solo shows at Superchief Gallery LA and exhibitions at Paradigm Gallery in Philadelphia, La Luz de Jesus, and Luna Rienne in San Francisco. She also built "The Sky Rabbit\'s House," a giant rabbit sculpture on a street-legal truck chassis. Her work has been featured in Juxtapoz, Colossal, Vice, and Hi-Fructose.',desc:'Three elaborately patterned pink flamingos stride across the wall on long dark legs, each bird filled with intricate floral and geometric details in shades of pink, coral, and lavender. They step over a blue-violet rainbow arch with rolling green hills beneath. Golden flower clusters bloom on the right side against a bright teal sky dotted with small clouds and birds. The piece covers the entire side of the building.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/bunnie-reiss.jpeg',from:'Landers, CA'},
  {id:39,a:'Chris Dyer',t:'Ride on the sPEACEship!',loc:'2875 7th Ave S',bldg:'Stewart Moving & Storage',lat:27.762825,lng:-82.672553,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'chris_dyer',bio:'Chris Dyer (b. 1979) is a Peruvian-Canadian visionary artist now based in St. Petersburg/Gulfport, Florida. Born in Ottawa, he grew up in Lima, Peru before returning to Canada at 17, eventually studying illustration at Dawson College in Montreal. A leading figure of the North American visionary art movement, his style fuses psychedelic imagery, Incan and pre-Columbian influences, sacred geometry, and 1980s-90s skate culture aesthetics. He has painted murals in 44+ countries, including at the Bob Marley Museum in Jamaica, the Beatles Ashram in India, and the STRAAT Museum in Amsterdam. He founded the Positive Creations brand in 2003 and has published two art books through Schiffer and Last Gasp. He teaches workshops at the Montreal Museum of Fine Arts, the National Gallery of Canada, and The Chapel of Sacred Mirrors (CoSM).',desc:'A panoramic cosmic scene against a starry black background on Stewart Moving & Storage. A wildly detailed spaceship made of interlocking creatures, tentacles, and geometric patterns blasts across the wall, trailing rainbow exhaust flames and lightning bolts. The vessel is piloted by psychedelic characters with multiple eyes and Incan-inspired headdresses, one flashing a peace sign from inside a blue bubble cockpit. "Positive Creations" floats above in rainbow lettering, "PEACE PLEASE" appears in a speech bubble at right, and a smiling sun face radiates in the upper right corner. Saturn, UFOs, and alien faces orbit the composition. Signed "Chris Dyer" at left and "LOVE!" in pink at lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/chris-dyer.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:41,a:'Fabstraq',t:'The Energy of Color',loc:'2724 6th Ave S',bldg:'Clay Coop',lat:27.7647,lng:-82.670595,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'fabstraq919',bio:'Fabstraq is the working name of Shawn Rainey, a New York native now based in Tampa. The name stands for "abstract art that makes you feel fabulous," and his tagline is "Abstract From The Heart." He\'s been making art since he was 10, when it became an escape from childhood trauma — experiences of isolation and rejection that he later channeled into a practice centered on healing through creativity. He\'s a certified Art Therapy Life Coach who volunteers at the Glazer Children\'s Museum in Tampa, teaches kids\' art classes at Center Place Fine Arts in Brandon, and visits schools across the Tampa area to talk about art and mental health. He works across painting, murals, and poetry. He was featured on Bay News 9\'s "Everyday Hero" segment in 2019 for inspiring children through art, and was included in the Green Book of Tampa Bay group exhibition at TheStudio@620 in St. Petersburg. He was selected as one of six local artists for SHINE 2023.',desc:'A bold, graphic mural covering the entire front face of the Clay Coop building. The composition is a patchwork of large geometric shapes — triangles, rectangles, and angular forms — in saturated flat colors: bright green, royal blue, hot pink, red, purple, yellow, and teal. A heart shape, a star, zigzag patterns, and concentric rectangles are scattered throughout the design. The overall effect is like an abstract quilt or a Memphis Design-inspired collage, with clean edges and no blending between the color fields. Two tiki statues flank the entrance, and the artist\'s "FABSTRAQ" tag appears in the lower right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/fabstraq.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL'},
  {id:42,a:'Greater Public Studio',t:'Dive Into Discovery',loc:'6th Ave S & 2nd St S',bldg:'USF Campus',lat:27.76005333,lng:-82.637055,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'greaterpublicstudio',bio:'Greater Public Studio is a Tampa-based creative team that designs large-scale murals and visual environments. Founded with a focus on making public spaces more engaging, GPS has taken on projects ranging from building-sized paintings to immersive installations across Florida and beyond. Their 2023 SHINE piece at the USF St. Pete campus brought a burst of color to the university district with "Dive Into Discovery," a vibrant scene depicting an imaginative community landscape full of whimsical architectural forms and figures.',desc:'A colorful, illustrative mural spanning a large wall near the USF St. Pete campus. The composition features a fantastical townscape with stacked buildings, winding paths, and small figures exploring the scene. Bright blues, oranges, and greens fill the design in a style that feels like an oversized storybook illustration.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/greater-public-studio.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL'},
  {id:44,a:'HOXXOH',t:'Rainbird',loc:'2025 3rd Ave S',bldg:'Morean Workshop Space',lat:27.76862,lng:-82.660675,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'hoxxoh',bio:'HOXXOH is the working name of Douglas Hoekzema, a Miami-based artist known for massive abstract murals built around concentric rings, ripple patterns, and optical illusions. He studied architecture and double-majored in Art History and Painting before turning to mural work full-time. He started in graffiti and evolved into a style that\'s hard to categorize — part Op Art, part organic abstraction, with a heavy emphasis on movement and rhythm. His signature "RainBird" technique uses modified irrigation systems to apply paint in flowing, controlled patterns at enormous scale, creating hundreds of thin undulating lines that give his walls the illusion of three- dimensional depth and motion. He\'s painted major walls at Art Basel Miami, Wynwood Walls, and mural festivals across the Americas and Europe. This is his second SHINE mural — his first was in 2015. His pieces tend to be enormous, often spanning entire building facades, and they have a hypnotic quality that shifts depending on your viewing distance and angle. He was named an honorable mention in Miami New Times\' MasterMind awards in 2016.',desc:'A huge panoramic piece spanning the full length of the Morean Workshop building. Three birds in flight — likely roseate spoonbills or herons — are rendered in HOXXOH\'s signature black-and-white ripple-line technique, their bodies made up of dense, undulating wave patterns that give them an almost holographic shimmer. The birds soar across a luminous teal and aquamarine background that itself ripples with flowing white and dark streaks, making the entire wall feel like it\'s in motion. The scale is impressive — each bird spans a full story of the building. It reads as abstract pattern from a distance and resolves into recognizable bird forms as you get closer.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/hoxxoh.jpeg',from:'Miami, FL'},
  {id:46,a:'Loretta Lizzio',t:'The Sirens Call',loc:'2875 7th Ave S',bldg:'Stewart Moving & Storage',lat:27.762869,lng:-82.67305,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'loretta_lizzio',bio:'Loretta Lizzio (b. 1986) is a Greek-Australian painter, photographer, and muralist based in Melbourne. She honed her artistic sensitivity through professional experience in publishing, photography, and advertising before dedicating herself full-time to fine art and murals. Her ethereal works layer oil, acrylic, resin, gold leaf, and intricate line work to explore themes of Greek mythology, romanticism, nature, and the female figure. She is the first female artist commissioned for the Hellenic Museum\'s Dialogue Series, creating "Well Behaved Women" — a three-part mural of fearless women from ancient Greek history. Her work has appeared at the Brisbane Street Art Festival, Vancouver Mural Festival, and Wall to Wall Festival, and in galleries including Haven, Nucleus, Beinart, and Outre. She has collaborated commercially with New Balance, Wacom, and Toyota.',desc:'A wide mural on the Stewart Moving & Storage building depicting three women as sirens from Greek mythology against a deep teal-green background. The figures emerge from lush green foliage, their pale skin overlaid with intricate leaf patterns and delicate line work that makes them appear to grow from the plants themselves. Their dark hair flows and intertwines across the top of the composition, creating a canopy effect. The sirens gaze outward with serene yet subtly unsettling expressions — beautiful and sinister at once. Signed "Loretta Lizzio" in the upper right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/loretta-lizzio.jpeg',from:'Melbourne, Australia'},
  {id:47,a:'Max Sansing',t:'Shining Hope',loc:'401 5th St N',bldg:'Trinity Lutheran Church',lat:27.777006,lng:-82.639969,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'maxsansing',bio:'Max Sansing is a Chicago-based fine artist and muralist who grew up on the South Side in the Avalon Park neighborhood. He began making art at 14 through graffiti, joining crews like Def Con 5 and Recking Krew, before formal training at the American Academy of Art. His style fuses the color-drenched dynamism of street art with the technical elegance of photorealism, working in oil, acrylic, spray paint, and enamel. His commissions include portraits of President Obama, Walter Payton, and Fred Hampton, plus projects for Nike/Virgil Abloh, the NBA All-Star Weekend, and Chicago Fire FC. He won "Best Street Artist" in Chicago\'s Best of Chicago awards (2017) and has exhibited at POW! WOW! Hawaii, Scope Miami Beach, and UNITY in Dubai. He is represented by Thinkspace Projects in Los Angeles and deeply committed to expanding arts access on Chicago\'s South Side.',desc:'A multi-story portrait on the north face of Trinity Lutheran Church depicting a young Black woman gazing outward with quiet intensity. Her face is rendered in photorealistic detail with warm brown skin tones, while her body dissolves into swirling, psychedelic layers of green, turquoise, purple, and gold — evoking geode cross-sections and topographic forms. A large hibiscus flower in peach and violet blooms at her shoulder, and deep blue flowers anchor the bottom of the composition. A red archway silhouette with palm trees appears within the flowing forms, and a bird silhouette flies above. A SHINE plaque is visible at the base.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/max-sansing.jpeg',from:'Chicago, IL'},
  {id:120,a:'Melanie Posner',t:'',loc:'',bldg:'',lat:27.772,lng:-82.6491,y:2023,cat:'commercial',ig:'therealmelpoz',bio:'Melanie Posner is an LA-based muralist originally from Pennsylvania who earned her BFA from the University of Tampa. She\'s known for vibrant, photorealistic portraits of women, and her client list includes Walt Disney World, Amazon Prime, and TikTok — for whom she painted a 40-foot mural in the heart of Hollywood. Her 2023 St. Pete mural features a woman\'s face in oversized reflective sunglasses with bright pink lips, set against a playful background of colorful polka dots on teal. The sunglasses reflect what appears to be the St. Pete waterfront, tying the portrait to its location.',desc:'A photorealistic woman\'s face fills the wall, wearing large reflective sunglasses that mirror a waterfront skyline scene. Bright pink lips and warm skin tones contrast with the cool teal background, which is dotted with oversized polka dots in pink, gold, red, and light blue. The piece wraps around windows that are incorporated into the design. Palm trees frame the mural on either side.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/unknown-1506.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA'},
  {id:48,a:'Michael Vasquez',t:'The Mangrove Refuge',loc:'2063 Central Ave',bldg:'Little Philly',lat:27.77136167,lng:-82.66161333,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'michaelvasquez_',bio:'Michael Vasquez was born in St. Petersburg, Florida in 1983 to a Polish- American mother and a Puerto Rican father. He grew up as an only child raised by a single mom, and that experience — the search for belonging, chosen family, and community among marginalized neighborhoods — runs through everything he paints. He earned his BFA Cum Laude from New World School of the Arts in Miami in 2005, where his professor Fredric Snitzer became a career-long mentor and gallerist. Snitzer took his work to Art Basel and the Armory Show early on, and by 2007 Vasquez had a solo show at the Kemper Museum of Contemporary Art in Kansas City. His figurative paintings depict young men in moments of camaraderie and tension — tattoos, bandanas, hand gestures, posture — treating street culture with the seriousness of anthropological portraiture. His work is held by the Rubell Museum, Perez Art Museum Miami, MOCA North Miami, the Dean Collection (Swizz Beatz and Alicia Keys), and Microsoft. He was included in the Smithsonian National Portrait Gallery\'s "Portraiture Now: Staging the Self" and "The Outwin 2019," and his painting "Chain Strangle" was featured in the touring exhibition "The Culture: Hip Hop and Contemporary Art" at the Baltimore Museum of Art and Schirn Kunsthalle Frankfurt. He also painted a wall at Wynwood Walls for its 10th anniversary. This is his second SHINE mural — his first was in 2015.',desc:'A large photorealistic scene depicting a group of young men hanging around a beige Datsun 280Z parked at a waterfront spot with mangroves visible in the background. One figure sits shirtless on the hood, another leans against the car in a white t-shirt, and others stand or crouch nearby — all in jeans and casual wear that evoke a timeless, late-70s or early-80s Florida vibe. The painting captures a specific moment of youthful camaraderie, with the car as the gathering point. The detail in the faces, clothing, and vehicle is remarkably precise, and the mangrove setting grounds the scene in coastal Florida.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/michael-vasquez.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg/Miami, FL'},
  {id:50,a:'Sara Sheppard',t:'We Are Magic',loc:'2336 5th Ave S',bldg:'New Dawn Coffee Co',lat:27.76661167,lng:-82.66522167,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'sarah.g.sheppard',bio:'Sara Sheppard is a painter, muralist, and metalsmith originally from Dallas, Texas who spent over a decade in St. Petersburg before relocating back to Texas. She grew up in an art-immersed household — her father was her mother\'s college art teacher — and studied photography, drawing, painting, graphic design, ceramics, and fabric art throughout school. After moving to Florida she built a business doing Venetian plaster, faux finishing, and murals for model homes and commercial clients. Her mural style draws from folk art traditions, mysticism, and ornamental design she\'s encountered traveling around the world — bold compositions on dark backgrounds with gold leaf effects, celestial imagery, and symmetrical designs that feel equal parts tarot card and Art Deco poster. She was commissioned by AARP and the Morean Arts Center for the "Life Reimagined" mural as part of the SHINE All Year program in 2017, and returned as a SHINE 2023 festival artist. She also does fine jewelry and metalsmithing, having trained at Creative Side Jewelry Academy in Austin — she initially enrolled to finish pieces her late father had given her, and it became a lasting practice. She was featured in the 2025 "We Built This City: Women Artists of SHINE" exhibition at the Morean Arts Center alongside 15 other women muralists who shaped St. Pete\'s mural scene.',desc:'A wide mural on a black background centered on a pair of large, detailed eyes gazing out from behind an ornate golden mask-like structure. The face is flanked by crescent moons, a starburst crown, and colorful tropical flowers — hibiscus in pink and teal. White feathered wings spread out on both sides, and elaborate gold filigree scrollwork curls outward to fill the edges of the wall. The words "We are all Magic" appear in script across the lower center. The overall effect is mystical and symmetrical, like a gilded altar piece reimagined as street art.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/sarah-sheppard.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:52,a:'蔡陈林',t:'The Path We Came / 我们来的路',loc:'1927 4th Ave S',bldg:'Lantmännen Unibake',lat:27.768364,lng:-82.659783,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'chenlin.cai',bio:'Chenlin Cai (蔡陈林, b. 1984) is a Chinese-American visual artist based in Philadelphia. Born in a fishing village in Fujian province, China, he holds MFAs from both Tsinghua University in Beijing and the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts (PAFA). He immigrated to the US in 2014. His work innovatively combines traditional Chinese ink painting with contemporary Western oil painting techniques, exploring the tension of navigating Eastern and Western cultures. Major commissions include "The Past Supporting the Future," a 54-foot crane mural on the Crane Community Center in Philadelphia\'s Chinatown created with Mural Arts Philadelphia, and an installation at Philadelphia International Airport. He received the "Excellent Artist Award" at the Eye Art 2017 exhibition at United Nations Headquarters and the Coverly Smith Prize at Woodmere Art Museum. He served as Commissioner on the Pennsylvania Governor\'s Advisory Commission on Asian Pacific American Affairs (2022-2023).',desc:'A wide mural on the Lantmännen Unibake building depicting three Chinese American railroad laborers rendered in blue-gray tones against a dramatic sunset of deep orange, gold, and purple. The three figures — one holding a staff, one reading a letter, one planting a flower with his hat removed — represent family longing, hope, and resilience. Behind them, golden mountain peaks evoke the Sierra Nevada, with a setting sun and crescent moon embodying the duality of aspiration and homesickness. A steam locomotive and train cars wind along railroad tracks at right. The mural reimagines Chinese laborers who built the First Transcontinental Railroad but were excluded from its 1869 completion photographs. Created in partnership with the James Museum\'s "From Far East to West" exhibition.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/chenlin-cai.jpeg',from:'Philadelphia, PA'},
  {id:55,a:'Ashley Cantero',t:'',loc:'2421 Central Ave.',bldg:'Fonda Nails & Spa',lat:27.77129167,lng:-82.6661,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'ashleycantero',bio:'Ashley Cantero is a Tampa native who paints murals, illustrations, and immersive installations steeped in \'60s psychedelia, Atomic Age design, and B-movie sci-fi. Her style is built on precise black outlines, bold color blocking, and characters that look like they walked off a drive-in movie poster — UFOs, aliens, and retro femme fatales are recurring motifs. She\'s a resident artist at Kress Contemporary in Ybor City, where she shares a studio space called DLUANCE with artist Juan Espinosa. Beyond murals, she built dioramas for FloridaRAMA (formerly Fairgrounds St. Pete), including "Swamp Motel" and "Drive-In," and painted an 8-wall retro-futuristic piece called "Cosmic Beams & Sunshine Dreams" for that venue. She was featured in the 2025 exhibition "We Built This City: Women Artists of SHINE" at the Morean Arts Center alongside 15 other Tampa Bay women muralists.',desc:'A retro sci-fi scene spanning the full wall — three flying saucers beam up figures against a teal background loaded with mid-century diamond shapes and starbursts. A suited figure stands on a vanishing-point highway while a green alien peers out from the right side. The palette is teal, pink, and chartreuse with heavy black outlines giving it a vintage comic book feel.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/ashley-cantero.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL'},
  {id:57,a:'Ben Johnston',t:'Rise',loc:'1010 Central Ave',bldg:'',lat:27.770697,lng:-82.649575,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'ben_johnston',bio:'Originally from Cape Town, South Africa, Ben Johnston now works out of Toronto creating typographic murals at massive scale. He\'s known for turning simple words into architectural illusions — his signature style uses isometric 3D letterforms with precise color gradients that make flat walls look like they\'re popping off the building. He\'s painted walls on six continents and shows fine art in galleries internationally.',desc:'A towering multi-story mural on the side of an apartment building spelling "RISE" in massive 3D block letters. The isometric letterforms are rendered in graduating shades of cerulean, sky blue, and white against a deep blue background, with crisp shadows giving the typography a sculptural, floating quality. Signed "Ben" in white script at the lower left. The mural dominates the building\'s full height, visible from blocks away along Central Avenue.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/ben-johnston.jpeg',from:'Toronto, Canada'},
  {id:59,a:'Dream Weaver',t:'',loc:'1510 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'',lat:27.771567,lng:-82.65477,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'dream.weavin',bio:'Taylor "Dreamweaver" Smith is a St. Petersburg-based street artist and muralist whose work invokes a sense of reverence for the natural world. Her evolving style integrates expressive abstraction and realism to capture the spirit of life through light and color — often featuring portraiture, vivid patterns, and striking color combinations. She creates murals, canvas works, and live painting activations for communities and brands both locally and nationwide.',desc:'A massive wall-sized explosion of color — a hybrid floral-phoenix form rendered in neon pinks, fiery oranges, deep purples, cerulean blues, and electric teals against a dark blue background. Feathered petals radiate outward from the center while swirling organic tendrils and tropical leaves frame the edges. Signed "Dreamweaver" in stylized script at the lower left. A SHINE festival plaque is visible at the base.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/dreamweaver.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:60,a:'Egypt Hagan',t:'',loc:'299 Dr MLK Jr St N',bldg:'George F. Young',lat:27.77535,lng:-82.6464,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'graffitiegypt',bio:'Egypt Hagan, who paints under the name Graffiti Egypt, is an LA-born muralist now based in Arizona. She\'s known for luminous, large-scale portraits that use bold color and gold tones to tell stories about identity and resilience. Her work has been exhibited internationally and featured in publications including the Sun-Sentinel and PRINT Magazine. Her 2022 SHINE mural on the George F. Young building features two faces in profile wreathed in roses, filling the multi-story wall with the kind of intimacy that\'s hard to pull off at that scale. The warm palette and painterly brushwork make it feel more like a gallery piece than a typical building mural.',desc:'Two faces in profile fill the multi-story wall of the George F. Young building, surrounded by roses and soft washes of warm color. The portraits are rendered with expressive, painterly brushwork — detailed enough to read as specific individuals but loose enough to feel like fine art rather than photorealism. Roses and floral elements weave around and between the two figures.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/egypt-hagan.jpeg',from:'Arizona'},
  {id:61,a:'Imagine',t:'',loc:'240 Dr MLK Jr St N',bldg:'RetroTiques',lat:27.77412,lng:-82.647112,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'imagine876',bio:'Imagine is a Kathmandu-born artist who fuses Sanskrit calligraphy with graffiti energy. Her large-scale murals turn ancient Nepalese script into repeating visual mantras — bold, rhythmic compositions that read as both sacred text and abstract pattern. She works across painting, sculpture, and public walls internationally.',desc:'A long parking-lot wall painted solid hot pink, covered with repeating columns of gold Sanskrit-inspired calligraphy. The metallic gold letterforms cascade downward in ornate clusters, with subtler red and pink swirls layered underneath. The effect is meditative and hypnotic — the same sacred text echoing across the entire wall like a visual chant.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/imagine.jpeg',from:'Kathmandu, Nepal'},
  {id:62,a:'James Bullough',t:'',loc:'2201 Central Ave.',bldg:'Central Neurology',lat:27.771253,lng:-82.663567,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'james_bullough',bio:'James Bullough grew up in Washington, DC, where early exposure to graffiti culture shaped his visual sensibility. He taught himself oil painting by studying Old Masters, then moved to Berlin where he now lives and works. His signature style splits photorealistic figures into fractured geometric ribbons — precise portraits shattered by bold abstract slashes. He paints both massive outdoor walls and studio canvases, with landmark murals in New Zealand, Sweden, France, Ukraine, and across the US. His gallery shows regularly sell out.',desc:'A multi-story mural on the side of a building depicting a woman in a crimson dress, her body fragmented into sweeping ribbon-like shards that scatter across a dark black background. The photorealistic figure — head tilted back, arms outstretched — appears to be dissolving or reassembling mid-leap. Deep navy blue, coral red, and muted lavender geometric strips weave through the composition, creating a sense of explosive movement against the void.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/james-bullough.jpeg',from:'Berlin, Germany'},
  {id:64,a:'Jeff Williams',t:'',loc:'511 Dr. MLK Jr St. N.',bldg:'Myoblast Strength',lat:27.77776333,lng:-82.6466,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'sunsigns.stpete',bio:'Jeff Williams is a St. Petersburg native who runs Sun Signs, a traditional sign painting shop in the city. His work is steeped in mid-century design — hand-drawn letterforms, 1950s advertising aesthetics, and vintage tattoo flash — and he\'s painted the sign for Sunken Gardens, one of Florida\'s oldest roadside attractions. He\'s been featured on PBS WEDU\'s Arts Plus. His SHINE 2022 mural at Myoblast Strength is a love letter to old Florida tourism — stacked horizontal bands reading "Florida Souvenirs," "St. Petersburg," "the sun... the sea... you & me," "Tampa Bay," and "Sunshine 360 Days a Year," all painted in vintage postcard style with retro illustrations. It looks like it could have been on a highway billboard in 1955.',desc:'A tall wall painted as a stack of vintage Florida tourism signs in bold primary colors. From top to bottom: "Florida SOUVENIRS" with palm trees, "ST. PETERSBURG" in large white block letters on black, "the sun... the sea... you & me — THE SUNSHINE CITY" on red with pin-up illustrations and oranges, "FLORIDA" in classic postcard lettering, "Tampa Bay" in red script with a pelican mascot, and "Sunshine 360 Days A Year" with a retro couple in green. Every detail is hand-painted in authentic mid-century sign style.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/jeff-williams.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:65,a:'MadC',t:'',loc:'1010 Central Ave.',bldg:'',lat:27.770712,lng:-82.649567,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'mad_c1',bio:'Claudia Walde, known as MadC, is a German graffiti artist and painter with nearly three decades in the game. Nicknamed "The Mad One" for her fearless approach, she\'s built an international reputation for translating the speed and energy of graffiti calligraphy into large-scale abstract compositions. Her work layers translucent sweeps of color that overlap and glow where they intersect — part street art, part color-field painting. She works on both massive outdoor walls and studio canvases.',desc:'A multi-story abstract mural on the side of an apartment building. Sweeping translucent ribbons of hot pink, cyan, orange, and white crisscross at sharp angles over a vivid yellow background. Where the color bands overlap they create new hues — purple where pink meets blue, warm peach where orange meets white. The effect is like looking through stacked sheets of colored glass. White paint splatter near the top right adds a calligraphic flourish.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/madc.jpeg',from:'Germany'},
  {id:66,a:'Marina Capdevila',t:'',loc:'601 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'The Sage',lat:27.771775,lng:-82.642442,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'marinacapdevila',bio:'Marina Capdevila is a Barcelona-born muralist who studied Fine Arts in her hometown and at the Willem de Kooning Academy in Rotterdam, where she first got into street art. Over the past decade she\'s painted walls across the Americas and Europe — from Brazil and Mexico to Belgium, Croatia, and the UK. Her style is playful and surreal: oversized, rubbery characters with exaggerated features interacting in absurd scenes full of warmth and humor.',desc:'A two-story wall dominated by a crowd of surreal orange-skinned characters in various states of mischief. On the left, a figure in a beret and sunglasses clutches a boombox. In the center, an enormous face wearing oversized reflective shades holds a lobster. On the right, a figure in a bucket hat dangles a fish on a line while a pale ghost-like creature peeks out between them. The palette is almost entirely warm oranges, creams, and golds against a yellow-gold upper band with tiny palm tree silhouettes. Signed "Marina Capdevila" at the upper left.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/marina-capdevila.jpeg',from:'Barcelona, Spain'},
  {id:68,a:'Reginald O\'Neal',t:'',loc:'701 3rd St. S.',bldg:'USF Piano Man Building',lat:27.76296167,lng:-82.63767167,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'reginaldo.neal',bio:'Reginald O\'Neal is a Miami-born painter who grew up in Overtown and trained under mentor Alejandro Dorda. His work has been acquired by Perez Art Museum Miami and ICA Miami, he\'s represented by Vielmetter Los Angeles gallery, and he was named to the Artsy Vanguard in 2021. He\'s completed murals in Austria, Norway, Spain, and the Dominican Republic. His SHINE 2022 mural on the USF Piano Man Building is a monochromatic composition depicting historian John Henrik Clarke in a suit alongside scenes of the Mundari tribe of South Sudan — figures with long-horned cattle and a fisherman working nets. The entire piece is rendered in blacks, whites, and warm sepia tones with classical oil-painting technique.',desc:'A sweeping black-and-white mural spanning the full wall. A suited man — historian John Henrik Clarke — sits as the central figure, painted with photorealistic precision. Behind him, silhouetted scenes show Mundari tribespeople tending long-horned cattle and casting fishing nets. The monochromatic palette and classical brushwork give the piece the weight of a museum painting at building scale.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/reginald-oneal.jpeg',from:'Miami, FL'},
  {id:69,a:'Sydney Prusso',t:'Look Inside',loc:'533 28th St. S.',bldg:'Seriously Strong Training',lat:27.765762,lng:-82.671425,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'sydneyprusso',bio:'Sydney Prusso is a St. Petersburg illustrator, lettering artist, and muralist. Her style pulls from 1960s and \'70s poster art — groovy hand-lettering, warm retro palettes, and flowing psychedelic compositions. Much of her work explores themes of mental health and the outdoors, wrapped in colorful, feel-good imagery that\'s impossible not to smile at.',desc:'The entire side of a peaked-roof building wrapped in retro psychedelic lettering spelling "Outside" and "Look Inside" in chunky, rounded \'70s-style typography. The letters are layered in pinks, oranges, chartreuse, white, and maroon against a purple background. Colorful butterflies — a large pink-and-red one at center, smaller ones floating above — are scattered between the words. Sparkle stars dot the upper peak. The overall vibe is a vintage concert poster blown up to building scale.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/sydney-prusso.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:71,a:'The Happy Mural Project',t:'Wild Ride',loc:'3001 Central Ave.',bldg:'St. Petersbark',lat:27.77132167,lng:-82.67402,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'alyssa.marie.of.the.sea',bio:'The Happy Mural Project is a collaborative mural initiative founded and directed by Alyssa Marie Everly, a St. Pete-based artist who grew up in the Rocky Mountains of Colorado. She earned her BFA from Metropolitan State University of Denver before relocating to Florida. Alyssa designs the murals and organizes groups of artists to help execute them — the project brings together various painters to collaborate on large-scale public works. Her designs specialize in nature and wildlife subjects, taking realistic animal forms and setting them against stylized, slightly impressionistic backgrounds. She\'s been leading mural projects professionally since 2017 and has done work for clients across the U.S. and internationally. She was commissioned by the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation for a mural celebrating the state\'s ecological connectivity efforts. In St. Pete the project has become a go-to for Florida wildlife subjects — manatees, gators, birds, and marine life on walls around the city. The Happy Mural Project is a three-time SHINE participant, having painted walls for the festival in 2020, 2022, and 2023.',desc:'A full-wall painting of an American alligator in profile, stretching from snout to tail across the entire building. The gator is rendered in rich browns, tans, and dark chocolate with detailed scale texturing throughout. Behind it, oversized golden sunflowers on green stems fill the background against a teal sky. The composition makes the gator feel massive — its head alone takes up about a third of the wall. The color contrast between the warm gator tones and the cool teal background gives it a Florida postcard quality.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/the-happy-mural-project.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:73,a:'Vitale Bros',t:'',loc:'3190 75th St. N.',bldg:'Parc',lat:27.77245333,lng:-82.643495,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'vitalebros',bio:'The Vitale Brothers — Johnny and Paul — are a St. Petersburg muralist duo who\'ve been part of the local art scene for years. They\'re known for large-scale character paintings with a playful, pop-culture-heavy approach that makes their walls some of the most photographed in the city. Their 2022 SHINE piece at Parc features a lineup of cartoon characters rendered in the Vitales\' bold, graphic style, turning the building wall into a crowd-pleasing photo spot.',desc:'A row of SpongeBob SquarePants characters fills the wall in the Vitale Brothers\' bold, graphic mural style. The familiar cartoon figures are painted at building scale with clean outlines and flat, saturated colors that pop against the building exterior. It\'s become one of the more popular photo-op walls in the area.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/vitale-bros.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:34,a:'Zulu Painter',t:'',loc:'Arlington Ave N',bldg:'CASA Family Justice Center',lat:27.7726,lng:-82.64923,y:2022,cat:'commercial',ig:'zulupainter1',bio:'Zulu Painter (Carlos Culbertson) is a St. Petersburg-based multimedia artist whose "Mindful Murals" appear on buildings across the US. His practice spans traditional painting, special effects, and scenic art, and he\'s been part of SHINE since 2017 when he painted "Thirst" for the festival. His vibrant, community-focused work aims to uplift through color and representation. The CASA Family Justice Center mural was commissioned by philanthropists Willi Rudowsky and Hal Freedman through a request-for-proposals process, completed ahead of the center\'s October 2022 grand opening. It spans two buildings on Arlington Avenue, two blocks north of Central, and was designed to create a welcoming environment for domestic violence survivors arriving at the center.',desc:'Four diverse faces fill the two-story CASA Family Justice Center building, representing different demographics affected by domestic violence. On one side, a woman cradles a baby, with words embedded in the curls of her hair — "Drive, Bravery, Family, Growth, Hope, Empathy, Love, Community, Wisdom, Faith, Create, and Unity." The other side depicts a young woman extending a helping hand and a young man making direct eye contact. Colorful geometric patterns, blue polka dots, swirling vines, and flowers in purples and golds surround the figures. A pathway of white polka dots and purple triangles crosses Arlington Avenue as part of the design.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/zulu-painter.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:74,a:'Aurailieus Artist',t:'',loc:'600 27th St. S.',bldg:'Zen Glass Studio',lat:27.76457833,lng:-82.66945,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'aurailieus_artist',bio:'Aurailieus is the artist name of Marko "Mark" Stevens, born in 1983 in Camden, New Jersey. He found art as an escape from what he describes as "the family business of drug addiction and incarceration," and his ambitions solidified in high school when a teacher gave him a book about Jean-Michel Basquiat. The name Aurailieus came from friends who started greeting him like Marcus Aurelius from the movie Gladiator — it stuck. He relocated to St. Petersburg nearly two decades ago and became a fixture of the local arts community. His most recognizable creation is Bob the Robot (Prince of Denmark), a cute yellow bot he describes as "a mascot for existentialism." In 2012 he co-founded the St. Pete Robot Exchange with Zulu Painter and Jason Bromley — an annual robot-themed group show that ran for nine editions and was even exhibited at the Florida State Fair. He won a 2020 SPAA Individual Artist Grant for "The Bob Project" to paint more Bob murals around the city. He had a solo retrospective at the Morean Arts Center titled "Fairly Local featuring Bob the Robot (Prince of Denmark)." He won the Open Call for SHINE 2021 and painted this wall at Zen Glass Studio.',desc:'A vibrant mixed-style piece on a teal cinder block wall. On the left, a female portrait with flowers in her hair and golden earrings gazes outward, rendered in a clean illustrative style. To her right, a burst of graffiti lettering in pinks, reds, purples, and blacks fills the center of the wall, with angular black wing-like shapes exploding outward. Scattered throughout are colorful elements — green and pink splashes, circular dots, and what appears to be a small yellow-green character (Bob the Robot). The piece has a collaborative jam feel, blending portraiture with wildstyle graffiti.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/aurailieus-artist.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:75,a:'Bakpak Durden',t:'',loc:'919 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'The Bends',lat:27.77227,lng:-82.64756667,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'bakpakdurden',bio:'Bakpak Durden is a self-taught Detroit artist working across oil, acrylic, graphite, and photography. Their hyperrealistic work fuses Baroque and Afro-Surrealist influences, and they\'ve shown at Cranbrook Art Museum\'s inaugural Fresh Paint series, Paradigm Gallery in Philadelphia, and Muskegon Museum of Art. They received the Torch of Wisdom Foundation Cultural Arts Rising Star award in 2024. The SHINE 2021 mural on The Bends is a dark, dramatic vanitas piece — a human jaw and skull emerge from swirling dark greens and blacks, with the text "TELL ME HOW YOU FEEL" visible in the upper corner. The entire wall reads like something out of a Baroque gallery, not on the side of a bar.',desc:'A dark, moody mural stretches across the long wall of The Bends. On a near-black background, a skull and exposed jawbone with visible teeth emerge from tangled, organic forms in deep greens and grays. The text "TELL ME HOW YOU FEEL" appears in the upper right corner. The hyperrealistic rendering and somber palette give it the gravity of a classical vanitas painting at massive scale.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/bakpak-durden.jpeg',from:'Detroit, MI'},
  {id:76,a:'Case Maclaim',t:'',loc:'2641 Central Ave.',bldg:'2641 Central Ave Condos',lat:27.77125333,lng:-82.66954667,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'case_maclaim',bio:'Case Maclaim is the working name of Andreas Von Chrzanowski, born in 1979 in Frankfurt am Main, Germany. He studied restoration at the University of Applied Sciences in Erfurt before turning to art full-time. In 2000 he co-founded the Maclaim Crew in Frankfurt with Akut and several other writers — the collective became one of the first to push photorealistic technique in the graffiti world, and their early jams in abandoned East German factories helped establish an entire movement. Case\'s technique is entirely freehand aerosol with no projectors or grids, which makes the level of anatomical detail he achieves at building scale genuinely hard to believe. His signature subject is intertwined hands — clasping, pulling, reaching — which he uses to explore connection, struggle, tenderness, and human fragility. He\'s painted walls in over 20 countries across Europe, the Americas, and Asia, and his work has been featured in galleries from Berlin to New York. He\'s widely considered one of the most technically skilled spray paint artists working today, and his work has been collected by institutions and private collectors internationally.',desc:'A massive photorealistic portrait of a healthcare worker in teal scrubs and a surgical cap, one gloved hand reaching directly toward the viewer. To the right, another pair of hands gently holds a small teddy bear sitting in a red toy car — a warm, personal touch against the clinical setting. The dark background makes the figures pop off the wall, and the skin and fabric detail is rendered at a level that reads as a photograph from across the parking lot.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/case-maclaim.jpeg',from:'Frankfurt am Main, Germany'},
  {id:78,a:'Emily Ding',t:'',loc:'100 7th St. S.',bldg:'Rob Graham Enterprise',lat:27.77026667,lng:-82.64296667,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'_emilyding',bio:'Emily Ding is a Houston-based muralist known for large-scale paintings of animals rendered with a classicist\'s attention to fur and feather and a surrealist\'s sense of composition. She\'s painted walls across the US — Sacramento, Oakland, DC, Salt Lake City, Flint — and internationally in China, and was featured in Houston\'s Mini Murals project and Michigan\'s Bright Walls festival. Her SHINE 2021 mural at Rob Graham Enterprise features a sleeping fawn curled up among white blossoms, painted in rich naturalistic detail against a deep forest-green background. The piece has a quiet tenderness to it, with the young deer nestled into roots and foliage as if the building wall were a woodland floor.',desc:'A young fawn sleeps curled in a gentle pose, its spotted fur rendered with painterly realism. White flowers bloom from the deer\'s back and around its body, growing up toward a window that\'s incorporated into the design. The deep emerald-green background gives the whole wall the feeling of a forest clearing. Emily Ding\'s signature is visible in the lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/emily-ding.jpeg',from:'Houston, TX'},
  {id:79,a:'Gleo',t:'',loc:'2336 5th Ave. S.',bldg:'New Dawn Coffee Co',lat:27.76637833,lng:-82.66497,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'gleo_co',bio:'Gleo is the working name of Natalia Gallego Sanchez, born in 1992 in Cali, Colombia. She started painting street art at 17, juggling chemistry classes during the day and fine arts at night, before studying Visual Arts and Graphic Design at Universidad del Valle. Her style layers dreamlike portraits of women and children with botanical motifs, pre-Columbian cultural references, and ornamental masks — everything rendered in soft earth tones and pastels with her signature glowing yellow orbs as a recurring element. She paints primarily with rollers and brushes at massive scale. Her 2018 mural "El Sueno Original" on a grain elevator in Wichita, Kansas covered 50,000 square feet and was recognized by the World Record Academy as the world\'s largest mural by a single artist. She\'s painted major walls across the Americas and Europe — Montreal, The Hague, Mexico City, Sao Paulo, Barcelona, Charlotte, and Rabat among them. Dinero, a major Colombian business magazine, named her one of their "40 Under 40" Colombians leading their fields.',desc:'A wide panoramic mural across the full building wall showing multiple overlapping faces and hands rendered in soft pinks and mauves. The figures intertwine with ornate mandala-like patterns in warm golds and oranges, with sunflowers and botanical details woven between the portraits. The color palette is dreamy — mostly blush pink, sage green, and amber against a pale background. The faces gaze in different directions, creating a layered, contemplative composition.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/gleo.jpeg',from:'Colombia'},
  {id:80,a:'Greg Mike',t:'',loc:'915 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'Goldman Wetzel',lat:27.77231167,lng:-82.64716333,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'gregmike',bio:'Greg Mike is an Atlanta-based artist and designer known for his character "Larry Loudmouf" and a hyper-saturated cartoon style that lands somewhere between vintage animation and pop surrealism. Beyond walls, he runs MADPOP — a line of designer toys and collectibles — and has had solo shows at ABV Gallery and painted projects for Moxy Hotels. His 2021 SHINE mural on the Goldman Wetzel building is pure Greg Mike — a packed crowd of grinning, bug-eyed characters in neon pinks and acid greens with "SMILE" running across the center. It\'s one of those walls where the energy is impossible to ignore from the sidewalk.',desc:'A massive wall covered edge-to-edge with Greg Mike\'s signature cartoon characters — bug-eyed, grinning figures in electric pinks, greens, and yellows. The word "SMILE" runs across the center in bold lettering, surrounded by the crowd of manic, playful characters that make the building impossible to walk past without looking.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/greg-mike.jpeg',from:'Atlanta, GA'},
  {id:82,a:'Jared Wright',t:'Harmony',loc:'670 Central Ave.',bldg:'Colony Grill',lat:27.77083333,lng:-82.64322833,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'jaredwright',bio:'Jared Wright is a Tampa Bay painter and illustrator whose style lands somewhere between Dali and an illustrated storybook. His SHINE 2021 mural on Colony Grill was his first-ever wall piece, and he was later selected for the 2024 Tampa Bay Rays Artist Series with work displayed inside Tropicana Field. The Colony Grill mural, titled "Harmony," features a dreamlike composition of an owl, a serpentine creature, and flowing organic forms in muted corals, sage greens, and teals. A moon floats among the intertwined figures, creating the kind of surreal, gently weird scene Wright is known for.',desc:'A surreal scene fills the Colony Grill wall with smooth, rounded organic forms in muted corals, sage, and teal. An owl with large dark eyes perches near a crescent moon while a snake-like creature winds through the composition. Leaf shapes, clouds, and celestial elements float through the scene, all rendered in soft, flat color fields with clean outlines.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/jared-wright.jpeg',from:'Tampa Bay, FL'},
  {id:83,a:'Jason Harvin',t:'',loc:'1211 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'Rick Noll Properties',lat:27.772442,lng:-82.650938,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'waywardwalls',bio:'Jason Harvin is a self-taught geometric abstraction artist who left a corporate career in 2022 to paint full time through his company Wayward Walls. His hard-edged style layers triangles, circles, and stripes into dense optical compositions that pulse with energy. Featured on HGTV\'s 100 Day Dream Home, his work can be found across homes and businesses in Tampa Bay. He\'s painted walls and intersection murals for SHINE across St. Petersburg.',desc:'A wide commercial building wrapped in interlocking geometric shapes — overlapping triangles, concentric circles, and bold diagonal stripes in saturated blue, orange, red, yellow, black, and white. A large red triangle dominates the center, framed by concentric blue-and-orange rings. A bright yellow half-circle anchors the bottom edge. Black-and-white striped bands slice through the composition at sharp angles, while smaller geometric motifs fill every remaining gap. Palm trees along the base add scale to the Op Art explosion.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/jason-harvin.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL'},
  {id:86,a:'Leo Gomez',t:'Can\'t Thank You Enough',loc:'744 6th Ave. S.',bldg:'Bayfront Health St. Pete',lat:27.76399667,lng:-82.64393667,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'leogomezstudio',bio:'Leo Gomez launched his eponymous studio in St. Petersburg in 2017, focusing on hand lettering, sign painting, and large-format murals. His client list runs from Disney Springs and the St. Pete/Clearwater International Airport to local spots like Green Bench Brewing and Tombolo Books. His SHINE 2021 piece at Bayfront Health reads "Can\'t Thank You Enough" in flowing gold script across a deep navy blue wall — a tribute to healthcare workers painted with the precision and flourish of a trained sign painter.',desc:'Elegant gold script lettering spelling "Can\'t Thank You Enough" fills the entire wall of a building adjacent to Bayfront Health. The flowing cursive letterforms are painted on a rich navy blue background, with decorative swashes and flourishes extending from each word. "Leo Gomez Studio" is signed in the upper right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/leo-gomez.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:87,a:'Michael Fatutoa',t:'',loc:'2060 1st Ave. S.',bldg:'Grayspeed',lat:27.769725,lng:-82.661583,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'samoan_mike',bio:'Michael Fatutoa grew up on the island of Tutuila in eastern Samoa and was raised in Hawaii before settling in Tampa Bay. A full-time tattoo artist at Sacred Tatau in Tampa, he draws on the ancient Samoan tradition of tatau — hand-tapped geometric patterns passed down through generations — and translates those motifs into large-scale murals. His work also pulls from broader Polynesian visual traditions including carvings and tapa cloth designs, bringing Pacific Island heritage to Florida walls.',desc:'Two massive sea creatures face each other on a flat gray wall. On the left, a hammerhead shark rendered in teal and black, its entire body filled with intricate Polynesian tatau patterns — spirals, chevrons, and layered geometric bands that follow the contours of its form. On the right, a sea turtle in black and white, its shell packed with bold angular motifs including step patterns and diamond shapes, with small teal accents at the flippers. The two animals appear to be circling each other, filling the wall from edge to edge.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/michael-fatutoa.jpeg',from:'Tampa Bay, FL'},
  {id:88,a:'Miss Crit',t:'Nowness is the Portal',loc:'3000 Central Ave.',bldg:'Absolutely Beautiful Flowers',lat:27.77072167,lng:-82.67395,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'miss_crit',bio:'Miss Crit is the working name of Laura Spencer, an illustrator and muralist born and raised in St. Petersburg. She earned her BFA in Illustration from Ringling College of Art and Design in Sarasota as a Trustees\' Scholar, and built her career right in the Burg, becoming one of the city\'s most visible local artists. Her style ranges from lush botanical realism to spooky, psychedelic character work — she\'s comfortable painting a giant magnolia on one wall and a third-eye skeleton on the next. She co-founded Artonomy LLC, a creative studio handling murals, illustration, and design. One of her most ambitious projects is the Sunshine City Mosaic, a 175-foot community mosaic made from over 15,000 hand-placed tiles. She was selected for the Dali Museum\'s "Outside In" exhibition in 2025, bringing her work into dialogue with the Surrealist tradition. Beyond walls, she does illustration, digital design, and interactive installations. She\'s painted multiple SHINE murals and has work scattered across the Grand Central District and downtown.',desc:'The phrase "NOWNESS IS THE PORTAL" stretches across the wall in large cream- colored serif lettering with a slight Art Nouveau curve to the type. Behind and around the text, a dense arrangement of tropical foliage fills the entire surface — deep green monstera leaves, burgundy ti plants, broad tropical leaves in teal and dark green, and a large white magnolia bloom opening in the upper right. The background is a moody dark teal that makes the plants and flower pop. The whole piece wraps around two second-story windows on the building face.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/miss-crit.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:89,a:'Mwanel Pierre-Louis',t:'',loc:'1100 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'Green Bench Brewing',lat:27.77184167,lng:-82.65008667,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'mwanel1_l',bio:'Mwanel Pierre-Louis is a Miami-based painter born in New York to Haitian parents. He studied at New World School of the Arts in Miami and the Art Center College of Design in Pasadena, and has since built a client roster that includes Adidas, Jack Daniel\'s, Foot Locker, Puma, the Miami Dolphins, and the Florida Panthers. His paintings have shown at Scope Art Fair during Art Basel and at POW!WOW! Hawaii. His 2021 SHINE portrait on the side of Green Bench Brewing features a woman with flowing locs against layered washes of warm color. The piece shows his signature approach — photorealistic faces dissolving into loose, expressive abstraction around the edges.',desc:'A striking portrait of a woman with long locs dominates the side of Green Bench Brewing. The face is rendered with photorealistic detail while the background dissolves into expressive washes of warm oranges, reds, and earth tones. The contrast between the precise portrait work and the loose, painterly surroundings gives the piece real presence from across the street.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/mwanel-pierre-louis.jpeg',from:'Miami, FL'},
  {id:92,a:'Ricky Watts',t:'',loc:'2622 Fairfield Ave. S.',bldg:'The Factory St. Pete',lat:27.76459667,lng:-82.66943333,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'rickywatts',bio:'Ricky Watts (Richard Watts) was born in 1980 in San Francisco. He started spray-painting at 13, published a Northern California graffiti zine called HelmetHeds for eight years, and taught himself fine art technique through decades of aerosol work — he briefly attended the Art Institute of California in San Diego for graphic design but left after two years to pursue painting. His signature style builds massive flowing organic shapes in saturated psychedelic color gradients, somewhere between liquid ribbons and biomorphic abstraction. He was selected as a Google Artist in Residence in 2021, painting a 10-wall lobby installation at Building 42 in Mountain View. He\'s done commissions for Louis Vuitton, YouTube, Mercedes-Benz, and IBM, and his illustration appeared on the 40-foot main stage at Outside Lands music festival. His 2013 mural on the Phoenix Theater in Petaluma was five stories tall, Kickstarter-funded, and remained the largest mural in Sonoma County for years. He\'s painted at POW! WOW! Hawaii (collaborating with James Bullough), POW! WOW! San Jose, and Wide Open Walls Sacramento. He currently lives in Sebastopol in Northern California. This SHINE 2021 piece was his 12th mural of the year.',desc:'A massive abstract mural wrapping around The Factory St. Pete building. Enormous organic, fluid shapes in bold saturated colors — orange, teal, yellow, deep blue, green, and coral — flow and overlap across the facade like giant brushstrokes frozen mid-motion. The forms are rounded and bulbous, somewhere between liquid ribbons and inflated tubes, creating a sense of constant movement. Palm trees in front of the building interact with the composition. The "Ricky Watts" signature and address "2622 Fairfield Ave S" are visible on the right side. The scale is impressive — the flowing shapes span two stories.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/ricky-watts.jpeg',from:'San Francisco, CA'},
  {id:93,a:'Woes Martin',t:'',loc:'2343 Emerson Ave. S.',bldg:'New Dawn Coffee Co 2',lat:27.765862,lng:-82.665778,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'woes',bio:'Aaron "Woes" Martin is an LA-based multidisciplinary artist known for his signature angry-eyed panda bears — part menacing, part adorable. Originally a graffiti writer, he evolved into aerosol muralism, acrylic painting, designer toys, and resin sculpture, bringing his snarling pandas to walls and galleries across the globe. He\'s also a DJ and puppeteer. Despite the ferocious faces in his work, Woes brings a laid-back aloha energy to everything he does.',desc:'A long warehouse wall covered in a pack of oversized panda bears piled on top of each other against a black background. The pandas are rendered in gritty black and white with exaggerated features — bloodshot pink eyes, bared fangs, and massive claws. Some snarl at the viewer while others sleep with one eye cracked open. Bright green bamboo leaves poke through the tangle of fur and limbs. The scale is enormous — each panda is taller than a parked truck.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/woes-martin.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA'},
  {id:33,a:'Ya La\'Ford',t:'Love Shines',loc:'255 Beach Dr NE',bldg:'Smith & Associates',lat:27.77573,lng:-82.632255,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'yalaford',bio:'Ya La\'Ford is a multidisciplinary artist working between Tampa Bay and New York. She holds an MFA from the Art Institute of Boston and a Juris Doctor from the University of Florida — a rare combination that informs her approach to large-scale public art commissions. Her geometric, pattern-driven murals and installations have earned projects with Nike, the NBA, and a piece honoring Maya Angelou, with permanent work at the Boston Public Library and the Dr. Carter G. Woodson African American Museum in St. Petersburg. In addition to painting, she teaches and maintains an active studio practice spanning installation, sculpture, and fine art. She has participated in the SHINE Mural Festival multiple times, and her bold use of interlocking geometric forms has become a recognizable part of St. Pete\'s downtown mural landscape.',desc:'A large heart shape on a dark charcoal wall, filled entirely with intricate orange geometric line patterns — angular, interlocking shapes that create a dense, woven texture within the heart. "Love Shines" is written across the center in flowing white cursive script. The Ya La\'ford signature and @yalaford tag appear below the heart, and an orange Smith & Associates Real Estate plaque sits at the lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/ya-la-ford.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:98,a:'Brian Butler',t:'',loc:'1101 1st Ave N',bldg:'MUV Dispensary',lat:27.77247,lng:-82.65004667,y:2020,cat:'shine',ig:'upperhandart',bio:'Brian Butler runs Upper Hand Art out of Miami, working across murals, illustration, gigposters, and live concert drawing. His "Show Drawn" series documents over 2,000 live performances sketched on-site at venues across the country. He\'s done album art for GZA, murals for Target and LAFC, and painted at POW!WOW! festivals in Worcester and Hawaii. His 2020 SHINE mural wraps the MUV Dispensary building in a sprawling nature scene — dense vegetation, birds, and flowing organic linework that turns the commercial facade into something closer to a botanical garden wall. The bold outlines and hand-illustrated feel are distinctly Butler.',desc:'A large-scale nature scene wraps the MUV Dispensary building, featuring dense tropical foliage, birds in flight, and flowing organic linework. The composition covers multiple faces of the building, creating an immersive green canopy effect. Bold outlines define the plant forms while softer color fills give the whole piece a hand-illustrated, almost storybook quality.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/brian-butler.jpeg',from:'Miami, Florida'},
  {id:101,a:'IBOMS',t:'Where the Bloom Begins',loc:'2324 Central Ave',bldg:'Grand Central Brewhouse',lat:27.77076167,lng:-82.66546667,y:2020,cat:'shine',ig:'i.b.o.m.s',bio:'iBOMS is the tag of Jabari Reed-Diop, a St. Petersburg-based artist who came up through Douglas Anderson School of the Arts in Jacksonville — one of Florida\'s top arts magnet schools — before settling in the Burg. His style sits somewhere between street art and cartoon illustration: chunky outlined characters with exaggerated expressions, heavy personality, and autobiographical weight. He pulls from his experiences as a young Black man in Florida and turns them into bold, narrative-driven compositions. His SHINE 2020 mural was created in partnership with the Pangeaseed Foundation\'s Sea Walls program, which pairs artists with ocean conservation themes — this piece addressed the red tide and harmful algal bloom crisis that devastated Florida\'s Gulf Coast. He received a Creative Pinellas Professional Artist Grant and was commissioned for an installation at Tampa International Airport. He\'s continued to build a presence across Tampa Bay with murals, gallery shows, and collaborative projects, and was one of the youngest artists featured in the SHINE festival when he painted this wall.',desc:'A large cartoon-style character dominates the bright yellow wall — a tattooed figure with blue and gray braided hair, gritted teeth, and an intense sideways glare. The word "BLOOMS" is tattooed across its chest. The character is surrounded by bulbous, cloud-like brown shapes that pile up around the base, with a wavy white line suggesting water and small fish swimming along the bottom edge. The IBOMS tag sits in the upper right corner. Stars and swirl details float above the character\'s head.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/iboms.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:102,a:'Kenny Coil and Marc Berenguer',t:'',loc:'1000 3rd St S',bldg:'USFSP Harbor Hall',lat:27.76005333,lng:-82.637055,y:2020,cat:'shine',ig:'kennycoil',bio:'Kenny Coil and Marc Berenguer are the creative duo behind Break Maiden, a Tampa-based design studio that works across branding, illustration, and large-scale murals. Coil grew up in the Tampa Bay area and brings a bold, graphic sensibility to their projects, while Berenguer moved from Barcelona to the U.S. in 2009, bringing a European eye for typography and minimal composition. Together they have worked with brands across every consumer category and built a reputation for clean, flat illustration styles with strong color palettes. Their SHINE 2020 mural at USFSP Harbor Hall transforms the building into a bright, mid-century-flavored celebration of Florida coastal life — herons, fish, mangroves, and downtown buildings rendered in cheerful geometric shapes.',desc:'A full-building mural in a flat, graphic illustration style depicting Florida nature and neighborhood life. The composition is divided into loose grid-like sections filled with stylized herons, egrets, pelicans, tropical fish, palm trees, mangroves, and colorful downtown buildings in teal, pink, yellow, orange, and sage green. The shapes are rounded and geometric with a mid-century modern feel — puffy trees with scalloped edges, simplified houses with bright facades, and birds rendered as elegant silhouettes. A large white egret dominates the lower right. The overall palette is warm and coastal, giving the building a cheerful, welcoming presence.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/kenny-coil-and-marc-berenguer.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, Florida'},
  {id:103,a:'Lili Yuan',t:'',loc:'Grand Central District',bldg:'ARTE',lat:27.771495,lng:-82.65477,y:2020,cat:'shine',ig:'oliooolio',bio:'Lili Yuan is a Chinese-born, Jacksonville-based artist who blends Eastern and Western visual traditions through hyperrealistic painting and muralism. She uses her own photography as source material, building large-scale works that merge figurative realism with cultural symbolism — often incorporating Chinese calligraphy, water imagery, and references to Taoist philosophy. Her mural work bridges fine art technique with street-scale ambition, and she has painted walls across Florida and beyond. She participated in SHINE 2020 in St. Petersburg and continues to explore the tension between her Chinese roots and American life through her studio and public work.',desc:'A large circular composition on a cream-colored wall depicting a woman with long dark hair floating in turquoise water, seen from above. She wears a flowing red dress with deep pleats that billow and spread around her like a blooming flower. The water is rendered with photorealistic light caustics and ripples. Her hands gently push against the fabric as her hair trails out beyond the circle\'s edge. To the left, bold Chinese calligraphy reads "shang shan ruo shui" (the highest good is like water) — a passage from the Tao Te Ching. A small red seal stamp appears at the bottom left corner. The @oliooolio tag is visible near the circle\'s edge.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/lili-yuan.jpeg',from:'Jacksonville, FL'},
  {id:117,a:'Palehorse',t:'Saraswati Goddess',loc:'',bldg:'',lat:27.77232167,lng:-82.64343333,y:2020,cat:'commercial',ig:'palehorsedesign',bio:'Palehorse (Chris Parks) is a St. Petersburg-based illustrator and fine artist who co-founded and curated the SHINE Mural Festival from 2015 to 2017. His highly detailed work draws on Southeast Asian and Latin mythology, and he\'s landed commercial projects with Nike, Under Armour, National Geographic, Hasbro, DC Comics, WWE, Red Bull, and Harley-Davidson. He\'s exhibited at Art Basel Miami and in galleries in LA, New York, Berlin, and Jakarta. The Saraswati Goddess mural, painted in August 2020 with his brother Matthew Parks and Cat Gomez, fills a building wall with an intricate depiction of the Hindu goddess of knowledge and arts. Saraswati plays a veena flanked by a swan and peacock, with an Om symbol and Devanagari script, all rendered in meticulous white and gold linework on a deep black background.',desc:'A massive black-and-gold mural depicting Saraswati, the Hindu goddess of knowledge and the arts. The multi-armed figure plays a veena (stringed instrument) at center, surrounded by extraordinary ornamental detail — a swan on the left, a peacock on the right, an Om symbol above, and the word "सरस्वती" (Saraswati) in Devanagari script. The entire composition is rendered in white and gold linework on a jet-black background, giving it the quality of an illuminated manuscript at building scale.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/palehorse.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:124,a:'George Rose',t:'',loc:'601 14th St S',bldg:'Campbell Park Handball Courts',lat:27.76297,lng:-82.65384667,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'george_rose',bio:'George Rose is a Melbourne-based Australian muralist who shifted to painting walls full-time in 2015 and quickly became one of Australia\'s foremost female muralists. She studied graphic design at the University of Canberra and fine art at ANU School of Art, and that intersection of design and art defines her approach — large-scale botanical and floral imagery rendered with bold illustrative outlines and signature spray- painted gradient fades in bright, surreal colors. She researches each mural location to find endangered or endemic flora and fauna, creating a link between the artwork and the area\'s ecology. She\'s painted walls across Australia, the U.S., Asia, and Europe — festivals include Bright Walls (Michigan), Sea Walls (St. Thomas), Darwin Street Art Festival, Wall to Wall, Brisbane Street Art Festival, Roskilde (Denmark), and KAABOO. Commercial clients include Disney, Instagram, TikTok, and Jansport. She\'s won multiple Resene Total Colour Awards and took the "Best Mural" title at the 2019 Street Art Australia Prize.',desc:'A sprawling botanical mural covering all eight walls of two back-to-back handball courts at Campbell Park. Large curved bell-shaped flowers big enough to climb into sit alongside massive fringed petals and tall striated leaves stretching floor to ceiling. The color palette transitions from golden orange at the top through pink in the middle to electric blue at the base, all outlined in smoky blue linework. Half the flowers are Florida natives and half are Australian natives, connecting Rose\'s home country with the mural\'s location.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/george-rose.jpeg',from:'Melbourne, Australia'},
  {id:113,a:'Morning Breath',t:'',loc:'250 Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. St N',bldg:'Coney Island Sandwich Shop',lat:27.774544,lng:-82.647133,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'morningbreathinc',bio:'Morning Breath Inc. is the creative duo of Doug Cunningham and Jason Noto, who met in 1996 while working at Think Skateboards in San Francisco. They founded their boutique design studio in 2002, now based in Brooklyn. Their style draws heavily from 1950s–1980s Americana — comics, Mad magazine, punk rock, hip-hop, heavy metal, and skateboarding. They use limited color palettes, bold lines, and vintage pop culture elements. Clients include Ray-Ban, Nike, Adidas, Vans, Beats by Dre, Jay-Z, and Foo Fighters. They have designed packaging for over 100 albums and won a Grammy Award in 2021 for Best Recording Package for Desert Sessions Volumes 11 & 12. They also painted a SHINE mural in 2015 at 2042 Central Avenue (since lost to development).',desc:'A 101-by-15-foot brush-painted mural in black, white, and red on a pale blue background, designed to look like a vintage comic book pamphlet held open wide. At left, the Steeplechase "Funny Face" from Coney Island grins with a missing tooth. The center features a sinister figure in a red jacket holding a "Welcome to Fear City" pamphlet — a reference to the real 1975 New York City scare pamphlet. Phrases like "GHASTLY," "STARTLING SECRETS of Mystery," "JITTERY NERVES," and "Picture You NERVOUS" are scattered across the composition in retro horror-comic typography. At far right, a large red devil face with curling mustache and horns looms above the wall. The mural was inspired by the Coney Island Sandwich Shop (operating since 1926) and contains subtle nods to New York City throughout.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/morning-breath.jpeg',from:'Brooklyn, NY'},
  {id:121,a:'Palehorse',t:'Temple of Eternal Bliss',loc:'2390 5th Ave S',bldg:'Bayboro Brewing',lat:27.76659667,lng:-82.66558,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'palehorsedesign',bio:'Palehorse is the studio name of Chris Parks, a St. Petersburg-based illustrator and fine artist who co-founded and curated the SHINE Mural Festival from 2015 to 2017. He earned his BFA from Ringling College of Art and Design in Sarasota. His work is rooted in Southeast Asian and Latin American mythology, rendered with obsessive linework detail that recalls traditional woodcuts and temple carvings. He frequently collaborates with his brother Matt Parks on large-scale mural projects. On the commercial side he\'s done projects for Nike, Under Armour, National Geographic, Hasbro, DC Comics, WWE, Red Bull, and Harley- Davidson. He\'s exhibited at Art Basel Miami and in galleries in LA, New York, Berlin, and Jakarta. In St. Pete he has multiple walls around the city, including both SHINE festival pieces and commercial commissions for local businesses.',desc:'An intricate Southeast Asian temple composition on a jet-black background. A central Thai-style pagoda structure with layered flame-shaped spires rises in the middle, flanked by two elaborate Naga serpent-dragons that coil outward in ornamental detail. The entire piece is rendered in fine linework using teal, red, gold, and white — giving it the look of a traditional temple painting scaled up to cover a freestanding wall. A thin white circle frames the central temple like a halo, and a trident symbol sits in the upper right corner. The level of detail is remarkable at this scale, with every scale, flame tip, and architectural ornament individually rendered.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/palehorse-bayboro.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:123,a:'DAAS',t:'We Are Going to Be Friends',loc:'128 20th St S',bldg:'Tri-J Towing',lat:27.76928,lng:-82.66040833,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'daas',bio:'DAAS is a muralist and painter whose legal name is simply DAAS — not an acronym or alias. He grew up in Jacksonville and St. Petersburg, Florida, and earned an A.A.S. in Graphic Design from Pinellas Technical College in Clearwater. Early in his career he worked as an assistant to an established muralist in Daytona Beach for nearly six years, learning materials, styles, and large-scale application techniques. In 2007 he moved to Osaka, Japan, initially contracted as an artist and performer for Universal Studios Japan. Living in Japan for roughly a decade had a profound effect on his style — exposure to origami and the precision-based mentality of Japanese craftsmen led him to develop his signature geometric faceted technique, which layers angular shapes like a cubist stained glass window to build up recognizable forms from flat color planes. After leaving Japan he settled in Austin, Texas. He\'s painted walls internationally across the U.S., Japan, China, Italy, Portugal, Jordan, Nepal, and the Dominican Republic. Notable commissions include a 60-foot mural for the Moody Center in Austin, the Van Cliburn Foundation\'s first-ever commissioned mural ("Chromesthesia") in Fort Worth, two 144-foot tunnel murals at Salt Lake City International Airport, and a series of murals in rural Chinese schools for Nippon Paint\'s "Color, Way of Love" project. His work has been featured in The New York Times, BBC, and Spoon & Tamago.',desc:'A full building-side mural depicting a group of rabbits rendered in DAAS\'s signature geometric faceted style. The bunnies are built from interlocking angular planes of soft grays, whites, lavenders, and pale blues, giving them a fractured, cubist look while remaining clearly recognizable. They sit in green grass along the base of the wall. The background is a patchwork of large triangles in warm tones — golds, olive greens, corals, and tans — creating a quilt-like geometric pattern behind the animals. The DAAS tag appears in blocky letters in the upper right corner. The piece covers the entire side of the Tri-J Towing building on 20th St.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/daas.jpeg',from:'Austin, TX'},
  {id:115,a:'Derek Donnelly',t:'365 Million Things To Do',loc:'54 7th St N',bldg:'Morean Arts Center (east wall)',lat:27.771672,lng:-82.644081,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'saintpaintarts',bio:'Derek Donnelly is a St. Petersburg native who graduated from Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001, then studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. He has been a full-time muralist since 2010. In 2011 he opened Saint Paint Arts gallery on the 600 block of Central Avenue, helping launch several artists\' careers before closing in 2014 to focus on murals. He later founded COVE gallery in Pinellas Park and Public Art Project Inc., a nonprofit promoting public art. His playful, illustration- meets-realism style frequently features his son Syre and Florida wildlife. Clients include AARP, Hilton Hotels, Tampa Bay Rays, MLB, and Fender Guitars. He is a multiple Best of the Bay winner and was selected as inaugural Artmaker 2026 artist at Ruth Eckerd Hall. For SHINE 2025 he served as Operations Lead and artist liaison.',desc:'A whimsical mural on the east wall of the Morean Arts Center depicting an anthropomorphic T-Rex dinosaur wearing a brown fedora, round glasses, and a blue-and-white Hawaiian shirt, holding a fishing rod over the waterfront. A pink-and-blue sunset sky frames a distant city skyline across the bay. The mural is part of Donnelly\'s AARP "Age Friendly St. Pete" series — the T-Rex going fishing represents "the older generation of St. Pete enjoying life to the fullest." Signed "Donnelly @saintpaintarts."',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/derek-donnelly-365-million.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:125,a:'Illsol',t:'Notice Us',loc:'2010 1st Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.77000333,lng:-82.66024667,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'_illsol_',bio:'Illsol is the collaborative duo of Tony Krol and Michelle Sawyer, a husband-and-wife team based in Tampa. The name combines their surnames while referencing the Spanish word for sun — a nod to Michelle\'s half- Cuban heritage. They officially formed Illsol in 2015 to focus on large- scale work. Michelle earned her BA in Fine Arts from the University of South Florida in 2006 and spent five years as an arts educator before completing a traditional tattoo apprenticeship. Tony comes from a stencil art and wheat-pasting background and runs CLRTY Agency, a design and multimedia studio. Together they founded Mergeculture Gallery in Tampa Heights in 2017, representing artists in urban contemporary and graffiti movements, and Heights Walls, a community mural initiative. They\'ve been commissioned by the City of Tampa for multiple public art projects including Dream Pushers, Faces of West Tampa, and the Tampa Convention Center. Michelle was selected for Valspar\'s 2025 "Be Bright" campaign at the Feeding Tampa Bay building.',desc:'A massive Florida panther portrait dominates the wall, its face rendered in photorealistic detail with piercing gold-green eyes, whiskers, and tawny fur blending into the surrounding abstract elements. The panther looms 22 feet high over the intersection. Behind and around the big cat, concentric rings in turquoise and coral create a haloed effect, while scattered teal dots, monstera leaf silhouettes, and fragmented geometric patterns fill the background. The palette balances the warm realism of the panther against cool abstract elements in teals, corals, and greens.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/notice-us.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL'},
  {id:114,a:'J&S Signs',t:'Point of Pines',loc:'553 9th St N',bldg:'',lat:27.778658,lng:-82.646439,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'jandssigns',bio:'J&S Signs is a vintage hand-lettering collaboration between Jeffrey Sincich (b. 1990, Belleair, FL) and Josh Stover, who met as ceramics majors at the University of Florida. After graduating in 2012, they began painting positive-message murals and transitioned into professional sign painting, founding J&S Signs in 2013 in St. Petersburg. They relocated to Portland, Oregon around 2015. Their style draws from 1930s–1950s lettering — bold, simple, with distinctive letter shapes and a signature mint-and-red palette. In 2021 they rebranded as "Variety Shop." Clients include Nike, Adidas, Patagonia, Airbnb, and the Portland Trail Blazers. Other well-known St. Pete murals include "You Are My Sunshine" at the Morean Arts Center and "Love Your Neighborhood" on Central Avenue.',desc:'A 15-by-60-foot mural in a retro, bold children\'s-book style using muted pink, mustard brown, sea green, and orange. The composition depicts three vintage St. Pete buildings: a green one-story motel at left with a 1950s neon "Motel" sign and an "All Are Welcome" placard; a two-story mustard post office at center with archways and a vintage "Oranges $1.99" sign; and a pink two-story apartment at right with orange and brown columns. The title "Point of Pines" references the Spanish meaning of "Pinellas" — the county\'s original name. Period details include a green bench, palm trees, and a layered skyline evoking mid-century Florida.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/js-signs-point-of-pines.jpeg',from:'Portland, OR (originally St. Petersburg, FL)'},
  {id:111,a:'Sara Sheppard',t:'Life Reimagined',loc:'719 Central Ave',bldg:'Morean Arts Center',lat:27.771703,lng:-82.644097,y:2017,cat:'shine',ig:'sarah.g.sheppard',bio:'Sara Sheppard is an experienced painter, muralist, designer, and metalsmith based in St. Petersburg, Florida. She draws inspiration from the people and places she has encountered around the world, aiming to convey beauty, romanticism, and mysticism in every medium. She also participated in SHINE 2023 with a mural at New Dawn Coffee Co.',desc:'A mural on the back wall of the Morean Arts Center framed with a navy blue border and ornate gold trim, giving it the appearance of a steampunk painting. At center, a woman merges with a banyan tree — her body becoming the trunk, her roots spreading into the ground. She reaches out to a red phoenix bird at left, symbolizing rebirth. The right side features massive interlocking bronze and gold gears representing the passage of time and the mechanics of working life. Teal water fills the base. Commissioned by AARP as part of the "Age Friendly St. Pete" SHINE All Year program in 2017.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2017/sarah-sheppard-life-reimagined.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:108,a:'Stephen Palladino',t:'',loc:'276 Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd N',bldg:'Benson Worley Architecture',lat:27.774639,lng:-82.646994,y:2017,cat:'shine',ig:'goodandshiddy',bio:'Stephen Palladino (b. 1986, Bronx, NY) is a globally recognized artist whose style merges street culture, fine art, and raw emotional storytelling. His father was a carpenter and pinstriper, his mother brought Sicilian heritage. By age 10 his talent earned him a spot in a magnet art program for formal classical training, but his true education came painting large-scale murals throughout New York City as a teenager. He left high school to pursue painting full-time. His signature distorted characters blend graffiti, cartoons, and German Expressionism into work that is "slick and gritty, dark and lowbrow." Collaborations include Lady Gaga, Tiffany & Co., Playboy, Absolut Vodka, Reebok, American Express, Capital One, and Hilton. He won First Place in Painting at the Beverly Hills artSHOW in 2016. Also known as "Good and Shiddy."',desc:'A 17-by-95-foot mural on the south wall of Benson Worley Architecture featuring a dense composition of distorted human figures and faces in bright fluorescent colors — aqua, orange, lime green, purple, blue, and rosy red. From a distance the forms read as a cheery abstract puzzle, but up close bold black line drawings reveal Palladino\'s expressive, German Expressionism- influenced style. Characters include an orange woman, a blue-suited man, and a red woman in profile, interspersed with phrases like "LOVE ME," "AIN\'T," "NOBODY but YOU," "LOOKIN NEAT," "GAS," "YO!," and "NOT TRUE" scrawled in handwritten text across the wall.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2017/stephen-palladino.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles / New York'},
  {id:112,a:'Alex Pardee',t:'Brightmares',loc:'251 10th St N',bldg:'',lat:27.774586,lng:-82.647125,y:2016,cat:'shine',ig:'alexpardee',bio:'Alex Pardee (b. 1976, Antioch, CA) is an American freelance artist, illustrator, and comics creator. Hospitalized at 14 while struggling with depression, art became his critical outlet. He started in graffiti and homemade sketch zines before publishing his first book "My Book of Colors" in 1999. His signature aesthetic — which he calls "Brightmares" — features fantastical, dark creatures rendered in unexpectedly vibrant colors. He illustrated album art for The Used, Aesop Rock, and In Flames, and worked on creature design for films including "Attack the Block" and "Sucker Punch." He served as Art Director and Creature Designer for "Digging Up the Marrow" (2014). Collaborations include Warner Bros., KidRobot, and Teddy Fresh. He is represented by Harman Projects and Spoke Art galleries.',desc:'A 46-by-19-foot mural depicting an emaciated, skeletal gray figure hunched beneath an enormous dripping concentric-circle orb in pink, blue, green, yellow, and orange — like a deflating rainbow balloon where the figure\'s head should be. The creature has elongated bony limbs and stands on bulbous white and tan blobs that spurt upward from the ground. A swirling black vortex fills the background. Colors drip downward from the orb as if melting in the Florida heat. This was the largest mural Pardee had ever attempted at the time, completed with assistance from fellow artist Ricky Watts.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2016/alex-pardee.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA'},
  {id:110,a:'Shark Toof',t:'',loc:'687 Central Ave',bldg:'State Theater',lat:27.771683,lng:-82.643142,y:2015,cat:'shine',ig:'shark__toof',bio:'Shark Toof (David Lew) is an American graffiti artist, stencillist, and muralist based in Los Angeles. He started painting graffiti in 1985 and is a graduate of ArtCenter College of Design in Pasadena. He is best known for his wheat-pasted images of a hand-drawn, gape-mouthed great white shark, which he uses as a symbol of "strength, optimism, and possibility." His work has been commissioned by Ritz Carlton, Samsung, Apple, Hurley, Burton, and Heineken.',desc:'A massive great white shark painted in dark gray and black across the east wall of the State Theater, a pink/coral building with arched windows on Central Avenue. The shark\'s head dives downward with its mouth wide open, baring rows of white teeth, positioned above a red metal-grate door. The piece was completed over four days using approximately 100 cans of aerosol paint on the 55-by-33-foot wall. The shark\'s menacing form contrasts with the ornate architectural details of the historic theater building.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2015/shark-toof.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA'},
  {id:126,a:'Sara Sheppard',t:'The Siren',loc:'600 27th St S',bldg:'Zen Glass Studio',lat:27.764575,lng:-82.66945,y:2014,cat:'commercial',ig:'sarah.g.sheppard',bio:'Sara Sheppard is a painter, muralist, and metalsmith originally from Dallas, Texas who spent over a decade in St. Petersburg before relocating back to Texas. She grew up in an art-immersed household — her father was her mother\'s college art teacher — and studied photography, drawing, painting, graphic design, ceramics, and fabric art throughout school. After moving to Florida she built a business doing Venetian plaster, faux finishing, and murals for model homes and commercial clients. Her mural style draws from folk art traditions, mysticism, and ornamental design she\'s encountered traveling around the world — bold compositions on dark backgrounds with gold leaf effects, celestial imagery, and symmetrical designs that feel equal parts tarot card and Art Deco poster. She was commissioned by AARP and the Morean Arts Center for the "Life Reimagined" mural as part of the SHINE All Year program in 2017, and returned as a SHINE 2023 festival artist. She also does fine jewelry and metalsmithing, having trained at Creative Side Jewelry Academy in Austin — she initially enrolled to finish pieces her late father had given her, and it became a lasting practice. She was featured in the 2025 "We Built This City: Women Artists of SHINE" exhibition at the Morean Arts Center alongside 15 other women muralists who shaped St. Pete\'s mural scene.',desc:'A nautical-themed piece centered on a golden porthole frame containing a mermaid/siren figure with flowing dark hair and pale skin, entwined with bright red octopus tentacles that curl through and around the circular frame. The porthole is set within an elaborate compass rose or ship\'s wheel pattern in gold, teal, and white, with geometric Art Deco-style rays radiating outward. The background alternates between teal triangles and white cross-hatched sections. The title "THE SIREN" appears in gold lettering on a dark plaque at the bottom center. The composition has a strong symmetrical, heraldic quality.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/the-siren.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:109,a:'Chad Mize',t:'Twiggy',loc:'653 Central Ave (alley)',bldg:'Behind Bluelucy Gallery',lat:27.771628,lng:-82.642617,y:0,cat:'shine-legacy',ig:'',bio:'Chad Mize is a multimedia artist often called "St. Pete\'s Godfather of Art." He founded MIZE Gallery (formerly Bluelucy Gallery) and was a member of SHINE\'s organizing committee. He has been painting Twiggy — the 1960s fashion icon — since 2003, citing her androgynous style, 60s aesthetic, and striking eyes as inspiration. This mural, painted circa 2014, is considered one of the pioneering murals that helped establish the 600 block of Central Avenue as an arts destination, predating the first SHINE festival in 2015.',desc:'A stencilled black-and-white mural of 1960s fashion icon Twiggy\'s face in the alley behind the 600 block of Central Avenue. The image references the iconic 1966 photograph by Barry Lategan, showing Twiggy\'s signature doe eyes with stars, rendered with an iridescent sheen against a faded light blue wall. The deliberately muted, pastel-hued palette contrasts with the bolder murals in the surrounding area.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/pre-shine/chad-mize-twiggy.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:116,a:'Chad Mize',t:'',loc:'421 4th Ave N',bldg:'Hollander Hotel',lat:27.777111,lng:-82.638869,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'chadmize',bio:'Chad Mize is a multimedia artist often called "St. Pete\'s Godfather of Art." He founded MIZE Gallery (formerly Bluelucy Gallery) and was a member of SHINE\'s organizing committee. His bold, colorful illustration style draws from pop art, comics, and Florida culture. He has multiple murals across St. Petersburg including the iconic Twiggy portrait behind his former gallery on Central Avenue.',desc:'A large three-story commercial mural on the side of the Hollander Hotel in Chad Mize\'s signature bold-line illustration style. On a white background, colorful vignettes in orange, teal, yellow, and pink depict Florida icons — flamingos, a pink dolphin, an orange slice, a mermaid, a seahorse, beach chairs, palm trees, and a retro couple in swimwear splashing in the surf. "St. Pete" appears in playful lettering at top, "HH Hollander Hotel" in gold at center, and "Florida" in a retro script below. NÜTRL Vodka Seltzer branding appears in the upper right. Signed "@CHADMIZE."',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/chad-mize-hollander.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:127,a:'Ernesto Maranje',t:'',loc:'2606 Fairfield Ave S',bldg:'Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation HQ',lat:27.76391667,lng:-82.66803,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'ernestomaranje',bio:'Ernesto Maranje is a Cuban-American artist born in Chicago in 1983 and raised in Miami\'s Hialeah neighborhood. He served seven years in the U.S. Coast Guard and discovered painting at 27 during his service — a knee injury kept him off boats during his last year, and he started by painting a California flag on a mechanic\'s shop window. He\'s entirely self-taught. By 2015 he\'d painted his first large-scale mural in Little Havana and his career took off. His style builds detailed, nature-immersive compositions where animals emerge from lush organic backgrounds of flowers, foliage, and painterly brushstrokes. He was selected to paint at Wynwood Walls for its prestigious 10-year anniversary in 2019, where he painted "Sentries." He\'s done commissions for Johnnie Walker (the Blue Label Miami Edition bottle design), Disney Springs, Starbucks, Chase, and Chewy. His international work includes murals in Beirut, Lebanon; Sulaymaniyah, Iraq (with aptART for UNHCR/World Refugee Day); a refugee camp in Athens; and three walls in Kyiv, Ukraine. He\'s also painted at Beyond Walls in Lynn, Massachusetts. His environmental work includes murals for PangeaSeed Sea Walls, Florida Atlantic University, and this Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation headquarters piece.',desc:'A richly detailed nature scene on the facade of the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation headquarters at The Factory. A great blue heron dominates the center, wings spread mid-stride, rendered in naturalistic grays, blues, and whites with remarkable feather detail. A Florida black bear peers from the left side among lush green foliage. A swallowtail kite swoops through the upper portion while the lower background suggests dark water and vegetation. The palette is moody and atmospheric — deep blues, greens, and stormy grays — giving it a more serious, conservation- minded tone than typical mural work. All the animals face the same direction, symbolizing the corridor concept of wildlife movement and connectivity.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/ernesto-maranje-wildlife-corridor.jpeg',from:'Miami, FL'},
  {id:119,a:'Matt Kress',t:'',loc:'1st St N & Dr. MLK Jr St N',bldg:'Animal Hospital',lat:27.77230833,lng:-82.64711167,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'mattkressart',bio:'Matt Kress is an Ohio native who studied art and graphic design at Kent State University, spent 16 years in business, then moved to St. Petersburg and went full-time as an artist in 2018. He\'s since painted 30-plus murals nationwide, including a piece for the Tampa Bay Buccaneers. He also created "The Mooney Anomaly," one of the world\'s first flying artworks, recognized internationally in aviation circles. His dogs-and-cats mural wraps the animal hospital at MLK and 1st Street North with a colorful, geometric pop-art treatment — a Boston terrier, a large dark dog, and a tabby cat rendered in bold color blocks against kaleidoscopic diamond patterns.',desc:'A long wall covered with oversized portraits of dogs and cats in a colorful geometric pop-art style. A Boston terrier, a large dark dog, and a tabby cat with prominent whiskers are painted in bold, faceted color blocks — blues, oranges, greens, reds — set against a kaleidoscopic background of intersecting diamond patterns. The style is vibrant and eye-catching from the parking lot.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/unknown-1503.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'},
  {id:107,a:'Melanie Posner',t:'',loc:'421 4th Ave N',bldg:'Hollander Hotel',lat:27.776736,lng:-82.638808,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'therealmelpoz',bio:'Melanie Posner is a fine artist and muralist from Philadelphia who earned her BFA in Painting from the University of Tampa in 2016. She specializes in vibrant, photorealistic paintings of women. Her clients include Walt Disney World (scenic artwork for Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge), Amazon Prime, and TikTok (a 40-foot mural on the LGBT Center of Los Angeles for Pride Month). She also has a SHINE 2018 mural "New Beginnings" at 4139 7th Terrace S. She was a cast member on the reality show Tampa Baes. Now based in Los Angeles.',desc:'An 18-foot-tall commercial mural on the back entrance of the Hollander Hotel near the swimming pool and tiki bar. A confident woman wearing a red head scarf and dark sunglasses holds a tall glass of beer, rendered in photorealistic style. The background features a bold psychedelic paisley pattern in mint green, yellow, and pink with black and white teardrop motifs. Signed "Melanie Posner" in the upper right, with "@muralcerveza" and "@therealmelpoz" at the bottom. Commissioned by New Belgium Brewing to promote their Mural Agua Fresca Cerveza summer beer.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/melanie-posner-hollander.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA'},
  {id:122,a:'Sara Sheppard',t:'Stronger Together',loc:'',bldg:'',lat:27.77120833,lng:-82.66300333,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'sarah.g.sheppard',bio:'Sara Sheppard is a painter, muralist, and metalsmith originally from Dallas, Texas who spent over a decade in St. Petersburg before relocating back to Texas. She grew up in an art-immersed household — her father was her mother\'s college art teacher — and studied photography, drawing, painting, graphic design, ceramics, and fabric art throughout school. After moving to Florida she built a business doing Venetian plaster, faux finishing, and murals for model homes and commercial clients. Her mural style draws from folk art traditions, mysticism, and ornamental design she\'s encountered traveling around the world — bold compositions with strong color blocking, stylized faces, and symmetrical designs. She was commissioned by AARP and the Morean Arts Center for the "Life Reimagined" mural as part of the SHINE All Year program in 2017, and returned as a SHINE 2023 festival artist. She also does fine jewelry and metalsmithing, having trained at Creative Side Jewelry Academy in Austin — she initially enrolled to finish pieces her late father had given her, and it became a lasting practice. She was featured in the 2025 "We Built This City: Women Artists of SHINE" exhibition at the Morean Arts Center alongside 15 other women muralists who shaped St. Pete\'s mural scene.',desc:'A wide panoramic mural showing a row of stylized faces in profile, each rendered in different bold color blocks — greens, yellows, purples, pinks, blues, and oranges. The faces overlap and interlock like puzzle pieces, surrounded by tropical leaves, palm fronds, and simple white flowers. The words "Stronger Together!" appear in gold script on the right side. The style is flat and graphic with clean geometric shapes making up each face, giving the whole piece a modern, poster-art quality. It reads as a celebration of community and diversity.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/sarah-sheppard-stronger-together.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL'}
];

